[narrator] Constructed in 1591,

485
00:20:54,688 --> 00:20:57,623
it withstood the attacks
of Sir Francis Drake,

486
00:20:57,690 --> 00:21:00,560
and witnessed many battles
throughout its proud history.

487
00:21:01,260 --> 00:21:03,397
-[Servo] Fun times at Guantanamo.

488
00:21:05,798 --> 00:21:07,033
-[Servo, Mike and Crow] Progress!

489
00:21:07,400 --> 00:21:09,435
[narrator] The streets
of old San Juan,

490
00:21:09,502 --> 00:21:12,405
once the cornerstone of
Spain's Caribbean empire.

491
00:21:12,472 --> 00:21:15,075
-Now the site of numerous muggings.
-[Servo] No!

492
00:21:16,310 --> 00:21:21,415
♪ Parsley, sage, rosemary and thyme ♪

493
00:21:22,215 --> 00:21:25,185
[narrator] Casablanca,
home of Ponce de Leon,

494
00:21:25,252 --> 00:21:27,120
who sailed with
Columbus and established

495
00:21:27,187 --> 00:21:29,122
the first permanent
settlement on the island.

496
00:21:29,188 --> 00:21:31,223
-[Servo] Oh, thanks a lot, Ponce.

497
00:21:31,758 --> 00:21:34,093
-And here's where the nuns sleep.

498
00:21:35,428 --> 00:21:38,298
[narrator] In the rooms and
courtyards of Casablanca,

499
00:21:38,365 --> 00:21:41,502
one can still hear the echoes
of the Spanish main.

500
00:21:41,568 --> 00:21:43,037
-[all] Whoa!

501
00:21:43,103 --> 00:21:46,038
[narrator] ...authentic costume, dance,

502
00:21:46,105 --> 00:21:48,442
and music of old San Juan.
-[Crow] Are not here.

503
00:21:52,678 --> 00:21:55,515
-[Crow] Valerie Harper lookalike
contests are held.

504
00:21:56,248 --> 00:21:59,987
-[Mike] Yes, no matter what the culture,
folk dancing is stupid.

505
00:22:01,220 --> 00:22:03,490
-[Servo] Here, Up With People get down.

506
00:22:04,657 --> 00:22:09,028
-[Mike] ♪ Don't break my heart
My achy breaky heart Puerto Rican heart ♪

507
00:22:09,095 --> 00:22:11,865
-Well, thanks for coming out
tonight and supporting live folk dancing.

508
00:22:11,932 --> 00:22:13,767
We have another show coming up, so don't--

509
00:22:14,267 --> 00:22:16,403
[narrator] The Museum of Art in Ponce,

510
00:22:16,470 --> 00:22:18,672
a perfect setting for a family outing.

511
00:22:18,738 --> 00:22:20,673
-[Crow] I wish we had that for you.

512
00:22:20,740 --> 00:22:22,442
[Mike laughs]

513
00:22:23,943 --> 00:22:26,680
[narrator] The virtuoso Pablo Casals...
-[Crow] Is porky.

514
00:22:26,747 --> 00:22:29,182
[narrator] ...founder of the island's
finest music festival.

515
00:22:29,248 --> 00:22:31,852
The museum collection
represents five centuries

516
00:22:31,918 --> 00:22:33,753
of American and European art.

517
00:22:33,820 --> 00:22:35,488
-[Mike] I'm not speaking to you.

518
00:22:35,722 --> 00:22:37,657
[narrator] The cultural institutions
of Puerto Rico

519
00:22:37,723 --> 00:22:39,625
enjoy an international reputation.

520
00:22:39,658 --> 00:22:40,827
-[Servo] For omelets.

521
00:22:43,930 --> 00:22:47,167
[narrator] All over the island,
abundant recreational facilities

522
00:22:47,233 --> 00:22:49,702
await the visitor, old and young.

523
00:22:49,770 --> 00:22:51,037
-Like swing sets!

524
00:22:53,307 --> 00:22:57,043
[narrator] Championship golf courses,
the finest to be found anywhere.

525
00:22:57,110 --> 00:22:58,245
-[Servo] Are in Florida.

526
00:23:00,547 --> 00:23:01,882
[narrator] The tennis season?

527
00:23:01,948 --> 00:23:03,217
Year round.

528
00:23:03,383 --> 00:23:05,552
-[Mike] Here, Gilligan plays a set.

529
00:23:06,787 --> 00:23:09,388
[narrator] Puerto Rico is
an indoor playground, too.

530
00:23:09,455 --> 00:23:12,292
There's nonstop excitement
both night and day.

531
00:23:12,325 --> 00:23:14,393
-[Servo] In Las Vegas and Atlantic City.

532
00:23:16,362 --> 00:23:17,630
-[Servo and Crow] Gambling!

533
00:23:19,967 --> 00:23:22,868
[narrator] With sunshine
360 days a year,

534
00:23:22,935 --> 00:23:25,138
Puerto Rico offers
something for everyone.

535
00:23:25,205 --> 00:23:26,540
-Like flights out.

536
00:23:26,607 --> 00:23:29,375
[narrator] Fun filled lifestyle,
whatever your pleasure.

537
00:23:30,010 --> 00:23:32,512
-[Mike] Like walking
or holding a tennis racquet.

538
00:23:36,015 --> 00:23:38,618
-[Servo] Spinal injuries are popular
on the island as well.

539
00:23:41,522 --> 00:23:45,458
[narrator] Graceful palms,
an abundance of beautiful beaches.

540
00:23:45,525 --> 00:23:47,960
Puerto Rico is indeed a vacation paradise.

541
00:23:48,027 --> 00:23:50,497
-Abundant with pink and white people.

542
00:23:55,202 --> 00:23:58,538
[narrator] Resorts like Palmas del Mar
blend old world charm

543
00:23:58,605 --> 00:24:00,107
with modern convenience.

544
00:24:00,173 --> 00:24:02,175
-[Mike] Aw, that means no hamburgers.

545
00:24:03,042 --> 00:24:06,378
[narrator] Condominiums, leisure
villages, and a complete range

546
00:24:06,445 --> 00:24:10,083
of outdoor activities make
Progress Island an ideal place

547
00:24:10,150 --> 00:24:12,585
to live for every member of the family.

548
00:24:12,652 --> 00:24:16,355
-[Servo] Except Mom, Dad, brother,
sister, grandpa, grandma.

549
00:24:16,422 --> 00:24:17,623
[music playing]

550
00:24:17,690 --> 00:24:18,792
-[Servo] Ooh!

551
00:24:19,425 --> 00:24:20,527
-[Crow] Hit me!

552
00:24:20,760 --> 00:24:24,063
[narrator] A band concert
under warm, tropical skies.

553
00:24:24,130 --> 00:24:26,465
-The music of John Philip Salsa.

554
00:24:30,903 --> 00:24:32,972
[narrator] For the people here,
the quality of life

555
00:24:33,038 --> 00:24:36,108
is being fulfilled
with each coming year.

556
00:24:36,175 --> 00:24:38,845
It has formed a continuing
pattern of progress

557
00:24:38,912 --> 00:24:41,715
that all started more than
a quarter of a century ago.

558
00:24:45,085 --> 00:24:46,085
-[all] A century ago!

559
00:24:46,152 --> 00:24:49,923
[narrator] In the beginning,
the economy was based on the land itself.

560
00:24:50,457 --> 00:24:52,225
From the time of Columbus,

561
00:24:52,292 --> 00:24:54,895
Puerto Rico has been praised
for the beauty of her landscape.

562
00:24:54,962 --> 00:24:57,463
-[Servo] And sacked and pillaged
many, many times.

563
00:24:58,765 --> 00:25:02,302
[narrator] Hibiscus and bougainvillea
flourish throughout the year.

564
00:25:02,368 --> 00:25:05,905
-[Mike] As do popincolicplooky
and flingilalalafingulala.

565
00:25:05,972 --> 00:25:07,307
[Servo mutters]

566
00:25:07,373 --> 00:25:11,143
[narrator] The mild tropical climate
encourages many forms of agriculture.

567
00:25:11,210 --> 00:25:13,247
-Here are some moo cows.

568
00:25:13,880 --> 00:25:16,348
[narrator] Green bananas
and young pineapples

569
00:25:16,415 --> 00:25:18,083
ripen in the Caribbean sun.

570
00:25:18,150 --> 00:25:19,752
-[Servo] Stop with the sexual imagery!

571
00:25:22,922 --> 00:25:24,892
[narrator] The largest crop is sugar
cane,

572
00:25:24,958 --> 00:25:26,927
which has been cultivated here
for centuries.

573
00:25:26,960 --> 00:25:30,563
-[Mike] So you can sugar frost your damn
corn flakes, filthy American pigs.

574
00:25:30,630 --> 00:25:34,902
[narrator] Special refineries
process the cane into sugar and molasses.

575
00:25:34,968 --> 00:25:38,405
And thus is born Puerto Rico's
most famous product.

576
00:25:38,505 --> 00:25:39,638
-[Servo and Crow] Mm?
-[narrator] Rum.

577
00:25:39,705 --> 00:25:42,008
-[all cheering]

578
00:25:43,277 --> 00:25:46,747
[narrator] The rums of Puerto Rico,
many different brands,

579
00:25:46,813 --> 00:25:49,415
all with flavors that are
remarkably light and dry.

580
00:25:49,482 --> 00:25:50,983
-[Servo, drunkenly] I love this job.
[hiccup]

581
00:25:51,050 --> 00:25:54,220
[narrator] Almost all of the rum sold
in the US is produced here,

582
00:25:54,287 --> 00:25:56,488
and its export is vital to the economy.

583
00:25:56,555 --> 00:25:58,658
So, drink rum constantly.

584
00:26:00,027 --> 00:26:03,228
[narrator] Within this building
is the heartbeat of that economy,

585
00:26:03,297 --> 00:26:05,598
the Economic Development Administration,

586
00:26:05,665 --> 00:26:09,135
which has achieved remarkable progress
through industrial growth.

587
00:26:09,202 --> 00:26:10,970
-[Mike] Here comes the hard sell.

588
00:26:11,037 --> 00:26:13,072
[narrator] Operation Bootstrap,

589
00:26:13,138 --> 00:26:16,777
a pioneering program for self-help
developed by the agency,

590
00:26:16,843 --> 00:26:19,045
has created an investment environment

591
00:26:19,112 --> 00:26:21,380
which attracts industry
from around the world.

592
00:26:21,547 --> 00:26:23,215
-You mean cheap labor.

593
00:26:23,283 --> 00:26:25,018
-[Mike] Even "sss-p-sss" is here.

594
00:26:28,220 --> 00:26:31,523
-[Servo] ♪ Strawberries, marshmallows
Tinfoil, and fudge ♪

595
00:26:31,590 --> 00:26:34,793
♪ And some dingle balls
Hanging from trees ♪

596
00:26:35,862 --> 00:26:38,765
-Why, even aliens from Mars are here.

597
00:26:38,832 --> 00:26:41,267
[narrator] A skilled workforce
makes Puerto Rico

598
00:26:41,333 --> 00:26:43,903
the largest manufacturer
of many computers,

599
00:26:43,970 --> 00:26:46,105
and a leader in solid state technology.

600
00:26:46,172 --> 00:26:49,142
-[Mike] Things with blinky,
buttony-type things.

601
00:26:51,010 --> 00:26:53,145
-[narrator] Pharmaceuticals...
-[Servo and Crow] Whoo!

602
00:26:53,212 --> 00:26:55,582
[narrator] ...that demand the highest
standards of quality and control.

603
00:26:55,648 --> 00:26:58,852
-When Judy Garland died,
it destroyed Puerto Rico's economy.

604
00:26:58,918 --> 00:27:01,755
[narrator] With the help of
a generous tax incentive program,

605
00:27:01,822 --> 00:27:04,157
hundreds of businesses,
both large and small,

606
00:27:04,223 --> 00:27:06,025
have grown and prospered here.

607
00:27:06,125 --> 00:27:07,593
-[Mike] Even the Pink Jeans Company.

608
00:27:07,660 --> 00:27:10,197
[narrator] Manufacturing includes
everything from apparel...

609
00:27:10,263 --> 00:27:12,332
[Servo humming]
[narrator] ...to high technology products

610
00:27:12,398 --> 00:27:13,633
for the home and industry.

611
00:27:13,700 --> 00:27:15,935
-[Servo] She's plucking her chin hairs!
Yuck.

612
00:27:16,002 --> 00:27:17,803
-Then KISS came to town!

613
00:27:20,407 --> 00:27:24,410
-[Mike] We don't know what this is, folks,
but it's definitely Puerto Rico stuff.

614
00:27:26,378 --> 00:27:28,615
[narrator] One of the most
important operations

615
00:27:28,682 --> 00:27:31,617
on Progress Island...
-[Servo beatboxing to soundtrack]

616
00:27:31,685 --> 00:27:34,353
[narrator] ...is petrochemicals. Nearly
two billion dollars has been invested

617
00:27:34,420 --> 00:27:35,422
in plants and facilities.

618
00:27:35,488 --> 00:27:37,657
-[Servo] And millions poisoned.

619
00:27:37,723 --> 00:27:39,725
[Servo beatboxing to music]

620
00:27:39,758 --> 00:27:41,527
-Another fun friend!

621
00:27:43,295 --> 00:27:45,932
-[Mike] Complete with giant rolls
of toilet paper.

622
00:27:45,998 --> 00:27:47,367
[narrator] Whatever the industry,

623
00:27:47,433 --> 00:27:50,870
Puerto Rico's greatest natural resource
is her people.

624
00:27:50,937 --> 00:27:52,538
-[Servo] Come exploit her people!

625
00:27:55,342 --> 00:27:58,878
-[Mike] Shoo-de-doo.
These will never go out of style.

626
00:27:58,945 --> 00:28:01,013
-Comes complete with
a Peter Frampton album.

627
00:28:03,315 --> 00:28:06,218
[narrator] A growing volume of goods
from the continental United States

628
00:28:06,285 --> 00:28:09,888
arrives in Puerto Rico,
now the fifth largest market

629
00:28:09,955 --> 00:28:12,525
in the world for US products and services.

630
00:28:12,592 --> 00:28:15,228
-[Servo] The fifth or 18th or something.

631
00:28:16,328 --> 00:28:18,765
[narrator] Customers here
on Progress Island

632
00:28:18,832 --> 00:28:22,168
mean over 130,000 jobs
throughout all the 50 states.

633
00:28:22,235 --> 00:28:23,235
-[Crow giggling]

634
00:28:23,302 --> 00:28:25,472
[narrator] And thus,
contribute billions of dollars

635
00:28:25,538 --> 00:28:27,640
to the nation's economy.
-[Servo] Oh.

636
00:28:27,707 --> 00:28:29,642
[narrator] Puerto Rico
and the United States,

637
00:28:30,177 --> 00:28:31,878
a partnership for progress.

638
00:28:31,945 --> 00:28:34,047
-[Mike] Whether you like it or not.

639
00:28:34,813 --> 00:28:37,083
-[Crow] Ahh!
OK, we'll move there!

640
00:28:37,150 --> 00:28:40,287
-[Mike] Can you dig it, can you dig it
can you dig it, can you dig it?

641
00:28:40,353 --> 00:28:42,222
[narrator] An America democracy.

642
00:28:42,288 --> 00:28:43,423
-[Mike] Are we starting over?

643
00:28:43,490 --> 00:28:45,258
[narrator] Island of old world elegance.

644
00:28:46,725 --> 00:28:49,462
Rich in its heritage
of art and culture.

645
00:28:49,528 --> 00:28:51,030
-Ooh, look at the art.

646
00:28:51,097 --> 00:28:53,633
[narrator] A wealth
of historic grandeur and beauty.

647
00:28:53,700 --> 00:28:55,168
-[Servo] Is found on other islands.

648
00:28:57,870 --> 00:29:00,407
[narrator] The Spaniards named it
"Puerto Rico".

649
00:29:00,473 --> 00:29:02,308
-[Mike] Because they couldn't
think of anything else.

650
00:29:02,375 --> 00:29:04,310
[narrator] Today, we call it...

651
00:29:04,377 --> 00:29:05,445
-[Crow] Cheap Laborville.

652
00:29:05,512 --> 00:29:07,247
[narrator] ...Progress Island, USA.

653
00:29:07,313 --> 00:29:10,850
-[Servo] Because we have no regard for
history, culture, or tradition.

654
00:29:10,917 --> 00:29:13,953
-[Mike] We would like to apologize
to all the people of Puerto Rico

655
00:29:14,020 --> 00:29:15,655
that we did not offend.

656
00:29:16,422 --> 00:29:17,423
Bye-bye.

657
00:29:31,570 --> 00:29:32,805
-[Servo humming]

658
00:29:32,872 --> 00:29:35,007
-Ha.

659
00:29:35,208 --> 00:29:37,643
-This is nice, isn't it?
-Yep.

660
00:29:37,710 --> 00:29:38,812
-Yeah.

661
00:29:39,945 --> 00:29:42,382
[rock music playing]

662
00:29:42,448 --> 00:29:43,617
-What the heck could that be?

663
00:29:43,683 --> 00:29:45,785
-I don't know.
Whatever it is, it's annoying.

664
00:29:45,852 --> 00:29:48,220
Hey, Cambot, show me
Rocket Number nine, will you?

665
00:29:48,287 --> 00:29:51,257
[cheering and rock music]

666
00:29:51,323 --> 00:29:52,625
-Hey, come on, keep it down!

667
00:29:52,692 --> 00:29:54,060
-[Servo] Yeah, you bunch
of water buffalo!

668
00:29:54,127 --> 00:29:55,228
-What's your problem?

669
00:29:55,295 --> 00:29:57,497
-Well, we were just wondering, you know,
if you could keep it down.

670
00:29:57,563 --> 00:29:58,732
-What do you mean?

671
00:29:58,798 --> 00:30:00,633
I mean, what's your deal?
Don't you party?

672
00:30:00,700 --> 00:30:01,767
-Yeah, party!

673
00:30:01,835 --> 00:30:03,670
-Woo! Party!
Yeah, we party all the time.

674
00:30:03,737 --> 00:30:04,770
-Come on.

675
00:30:04,837 --> 00:30:06,738
-Because you can come over
anytime, and it's no big deal.

676
00:30:06,805 --> 00:30:08,675
-Really?
-You know what, you guys?

677
00:30:08,742 --> 00:30:10,877
I was just thinking,
when we graduate,

678
00:30:10,943 --> 00:30:12,778
I'm not gonna see
you guys anymore.

679
00:30:12,845 --> 00:30:14,847
And I love you guys so much.

680
00:30:14,913 --> 00:30:16,048
-Shut up, Shelly!

681
00:30:16,115 --> 00:30:19,118
-Well, we were just wondering, you know,
if you could pipe down.

682
00:30:19,185 --> 00:30:20,320
-Pipe!
-[cheering]

683
00:30:20,387 --> 00:30:21,720
-Bad choice of words, Mike.

684
00:30:21,787 --> 00:30:25,023
-Oh, my God, you guys, Doug gets off
in about a half an hour

685
00:30:25,090 --> 00:30:27,127
at the Pump and Munch.
I gotta get ready, all right?

686
00:30:27,160 --> 00:30:28,227
Keep partying!

687
00:30:28,293 --> 00:30:30,930
-Hey, here's my two bucks!
Who do I pay?

688
00:30:30,997 --> 00:30:32,732
-[groan]
-What the hell?

689
00:30:32,798 --> 00:30:36,468
-Hey, you wanna stick around
and party after everyone goes?

690
00:30:36,535 --> 00:30:37,903
-Hey, come on over, guys.

691
00:30:37,970 --> 00:30:39,738
They got Rick Wakeman's
Journey to the Center of the Earth!

692
00:30:39,805 --> 00:30:40,807
[cheering]

693
00:30:40,873 --> 00:30:43,643
-Crow, come on. Get back here.
This is so depressing.

694
00:30:43,710 --> 00:30:45,110
This is like
something out of our--

695
00:30:45,177 --> 00:30:46,345
-Out of our youth, maybe?

696
00:30:46,412 --> 00:30:47,613
I know just what to do, Mike.

697
00:30:47,680 --> 00:30:49,348
[whistling siren noises]

698
00:30:49,415 --> 00:30:50,817
-I smell bacon!

699
00:30:50,883 --> 00:30:52,152
Escape!

700
00:30:52,818 --> 00:30:54,487
-[laughing]
-That's pretty good.

701
00:30:54,553 --> 00:30:57,557
-Whoa. Geez, Mike, what have
you got against partying?

702
00:30:57,623 --> 00:30:59,225
-Hey, I party--
We'll be right back.

703
00:30:59,292 --> 00:31:00,927
I party as much as the next guy.
-Look what I got.

704
00:31:00,960 --> 00:31:02,328
-You got a tattoo!
-Yeah!

705
00:31:08,400 --> 00:31:10,168
[ticking clock]

706
00:31:10,237 --> 00:31:12,705
-[Crow] I was having so much fun.
-[Servo] Wild man.

707
00:31:12,772 --> 00:31:14,273
-[Mike] Well, maybe we'll party
sometime.

708
00:31:14,340 --> 00:31:17,877
-[Servo] Shabbily furnished room action
like you've never seen it before.

709
00:31:19,278 --> 00:31:20,647
-I'm Mike Wallace.

710
00:31:20,713 --> 00:31:21,948
-[Mike] I'm Morley Safer.

711
00:31:22,015 --> 00:31:23,348
-[Servo] I'm Ed Bradley.

712
00:31:24,183 --> 00:31:27,453
- Odds are she's eating
ramen noodles tonight.

713
00:31:27,953 --> 00:31:29,922
-[Mike] ♪ Woke up, got out of bed ♪

714
00:31:29,988 --> 00:31:33,492
♪ Dragged a comb across my head ♪

715
00:31:33,993 --> 00:31:36,662
-I'd be depressed
were it not for my room.

716
00:31:40,933 --> 00:31:42,135
-[Servo] Joey Ramone!

717
00:31:43,603 --> 00:31:45,572
-[Mike] Well, it's really
not worth screaming.

718
00:31:45,638 --> 00:31:46,872
What's the point?

719
00:31:48,273 --> 00:31:50,543
-The Cindy Williams murders.

720
00:31:52,878 --> 00:31:54,847
-[Mike] OK, I'll give you my bio notes!

721
00:31:57,517 --> 00:31:58,652
[ticking stops]

722
00:31:58,985 --> 00:32:01,220
-[Mike] Oh, it was her who was ticking.

723
00:32:03,222 --> 00:32:05,992
Let me tuck you in.
You've had a hard day of dying.

724
00:32:08,695 --> 00:32:10,830
Just, uh, show the folks my butt there.

725
00:32:14,533 --> 00:32:16,302
-[Servo] Presents, Scarlet.

726
00:32:16,970 --> 00:32:18,503
Maybe not. I don't think so.

727
00:32:19,938 --> 00:32:22,642
-[Mike] Abbot and Costello meet
the Beast of Yucca Flats.

728
00:32:22,708 --> 00:32:25,210
-I wish it was
the breast of Yucca Flats.

729
00:32:27,880 --> 00:32:30,182
-[Servo] You think Coleman Francis
had a casting couch?

730
00:32:30,248 --> 00:32:32,485
-[Mike] Probably a casting cot.

731
00:32:33,787 --> 00:32:34,887
Lost Boys?

732
00:32:40,827 --> 00:32:44,430
-Oh, so the beast
pilot's in in his own Piper Cub.

733
00:32:48,300 --> 00:32:49,935
-[Mike] Oh.
-[Servo] Huh.

734
00:32:50,002 --> 00:32:52,205
I figured Tor Johnson
would play the butler.

735
00:32:52,338 --> 00:32:53,840
-[Mike] Tor Johnson as the Beast.

736
00:32:53,907 --> 00:32:55,408
That's just smart casting.

737
00:32:55,475 --> 00:32:56,575
-[Crow] Mm.

738
00:32:56,642 --> 00:32:59,412
Coleman Francis actually
had a staff for this movie.

739
00:32:59,478 --> 00:33:00,747
Can you believe it?

740
00:33:00,813 --> 00:33:02,715
-[Servo] Ah, Progress Island.

741
00:33:03,750 --> 00:33:05,885
-[Mike] Get off my land, you credits.

742
00:33:07,887 --> 00:33:11,357
-[Servo] ♪ Never be
Your Beast of Yucca Flats ♪

743
00:33:11,657 --> 00:33:12,758
Thank you.

744
00:33:16,528 --> 00:33:18,832
-[Mike] Masterpiece Theater presents.

745
00:33:20,232 --> 00:33:23,035
-When in Hollywood,
visit Francisland.

746
00:33:24,903 --> 00:33:27,907
-[Servo] This doesn't look like
the Coleman Francis genre, does it?

747
00:33:27,973 --> 00:33:30,877
[narrator] Joseph Javorsky,
noted scientist.

748
00:33:30,943 --> 00:33:31,977
-[Mike] And airplane.

749
00:33:32,045 --> 00:33:35,013
[narrator] Recently escaped
from behind the Iron Curtain.

750
00:33:35,080 --> 00:33:37,417
Wife and children killed in Hungary.

751
00:33:37,883 --> 00:33:39,885
His aide carries a briefcase--

752
00:33:39,952 --> 00:33:42,255
secret data on the Russian moon shot.

753
00:33:42,322 --> 00:33:43,455
-[Crow] And a change of underpants.

754
00:33:43,522 --> 00:33:47,427
[narrator] Joseph Javorsky's
destination-- Yucca Flats,

755
00:33:47,527 --> 00:33:50,930
and a meeting with top brass
at the A-bomb testing grounds.

756
00:33:51,030 --> 00:33:52,932
-[Servo] Avon testing grounds?
-[Crow] Huh.

757
00:33:52,998 --> 00:33:54,700
[Servo] Rabbits with little shaved
butts.

758
00:33:56,702 --> 00:33:59,772
-[Mike] Oh, wait. I'm sorry.
That's just Tor Johnson.

759
00:34:00,973 --> 00:34:02,742
-[Crow] Mitch Jenkins pulls up.

760
00:34:02,808 --> 00:34:05,612
Destination, Yucca Flats Super Red.

761
00:34:07,380 --> 00:34:10,182
-[Servo] Time for go
to ground transportation.

762
00:34:11,550 --> 00:34:12,818
-Here's the airport, Mr. Khrushchev.

763
00:34:12,885 --> 00:34:14,887
Well, not really an airport.
More of a field.

764
00:34:14,953 --> 00:34:17,022
A landing strip.
Well, not a strip.

765
00:34:17,090 --> 00:34:19,958
-[Mike] Marlon Brando arrives for
the Larry King interview.

766
00:34:20,025 --> 00:34:21,627
-[Servo] Jeez, even his head
has a beer belly.

767
00:34:21,693 --> 00:34:24,030
[narrator] These men are also
from behind the Iron Curtain.

768
00:34:24,063 --> 00:34:25,163
-Pay no attention to them.

769
00:34:25,230 --> 00:34:28,433
[narrator] Two of the Kremlin's
most ruthless agents.

770
00:34:28,500 --> 00:34:29,802
Their orders?

771
00:34:30,770 --> 00:34:32,405
Get the briefcase.

772
00:34:33,438 --> 00:34:34,673
Kill Javorsky.

773
00:34:34,740 --> 00:34:35,908
-[Mike] They got it all mixed up.

774
00:34:35,975 --> 00:34:38,778
They killed the briefcase
and got Divorsky.

775
00:34:41,213 --> 00:34:42,748
-[Servo] Oh, it's a divining gun.

776
00:34:46,252 --> 00:34:48,420
[airplane flies overhead]

777
00:34:48,487 --> 00:34:50,623
-[Mike] Daddy, what's Vietnam?

778
00:34:51,123 --> 00:34:53,392
-Ooh, he's got a high albedo.

779
00:34:55,227 --> 00:34:56,395
-[Servo] Tony Cardoza!
-[gunshots]

780
00:34:56,462 --> 00:34:58,263
-[Crow] Ew, ew, ew!

781
00:35:00,800 --> 00:35:03,302
-[Mike] This here part doesn't
need any narration, folks.

782
00:35:03,368 --> 00:35:05,170
It stands on its own.

783
00:35:06,805 --> 00:35:07,840
[gunshot]

784
00:35:07,907 --> 00:35:09,342
-[man] Mr. Javorsky, get in the car.

785
00:35:09,408 --> 00:35:11,977
-[Servo] I'm off-camera,
but just do as I say.

786
00:35:14,280 --> 00:35:17,115
-Haven't really
caught the wave of this movie yet.

787
00:35:20,285 --> 00:35:22,522
-[Mike] Who's shooting who
and what should we do?

788
00:35:25,558 --> 00:35:28,260
-[Servo] Beautiful.
Just beautiful.

789
00:35:28,327 --> 00:35:31,430
Off camera excitement,
the Coleman Francis way.

790
00:35:36,735 --> 00:35:39,305
-Johnny, you forgot your lunch!

791
00:35:40,438 --> 00:35:41,740
-[Mike gasps] The coffee guy!

792
00:35:41,807 --> 00:35:42,875
-[Servo] I like coffee.

793
00:35:46,578 --> 00:35:48,747
-They're driving
through a Wisconsin petting zoo!

794
00:35:51,750 --> 00:35:54,153
-[Mike bleating] Help, please.

795
00:35:54,853 --> 00:35:55,955
Help.

796
00:35:56,722 --> 00:35:58,958
-[Servo] Ah, and what would
a Coleman Francis movie be

797
00:35:59,025 --> 00:36:00,993
without the Coleman Francis mountain?

798
00:36:03,328 --> 00:36:06,265
-[Crow] It is more suspenseful
when you don't know what's going on.

799
00:36:11,603 --> 00:36:13,072
[narrator] Flag on the moon.

800
00:36:13,873 --> 00:36:15,240
How did it get there?

801
00:36:15,642 --> 00:36:18,477
-[Mike] These are all just
random sentences, folks.

802
00:36:19,678 --> 00:36:21,747
-[Servo] This does have
all the earmarks of a KGB operation.

803
00:36:21,813 --> 00:36:24,383
[narrator] Secret data.
Pictures of the moon.

804
00:36:24,450 --> 00:36:25,585
-Nude.

805
00:36:26,752 --> 00:36:28,053
-[Mike] I'm ruthless!

806
00:36:29,422 --> 00:36:33,325
[narrator] Secret data,
never before outside the Kremlin.

807
00:36:33,858 --> 00:36:35,862
Man's first rocket to the moon.

808
00:36:36,295 --> 00:36:38,163
-It's the KGB, Mr. Benny!

809
00:36:41,667 --> 00:36:43,402
-[Mike] He's either a ruthless agent

810
00:36:43,468 --> 00:36:45,437
or he's Schemer from
Shining Time Station.

811
00:36:51,877 --> 00:36:54,713
-[Servo] I just love how deep Tony
Cardoza gets into character.

812
00:36:54,780 --> 00:36:57,182
-Yeah, he just goes in there and sits.

813
00:36:57,250 --> 00:36:59,918
-[Mike] He did all his own fake driving
for this movie.

814
00:37:10,362 --> 00:37:13,632
-[Crow] OK, you chase me
for a while. [giggling]

815
00:37:13,698 --> 00:37:15,267
-[Mike] A star city.

816
00:37:19,105 --> 00:37:21,373
[gunshots]

817
00:37:22,007 --> 00:37:23,142
-[Crow] Beautiful.

818
00:37:25,577 --> 00:37:28,913
-[Mike] Coleman Francis,
the cinematic poet of parking.

819
00:37:31,117 --> 00:37:32,117
I'm ruthless!

820
00:37:32,183 --> 00:37:34,920
[gunshots]

821
00:37:36,055 --> 00:37:39,092
[robots and Mike laughing]
-[Crow] I call him dead!

822
00:37:40,458 --> 00:37:42,295
[gunshots]

823
00:37:42,362 --> 00:37:45,563
-[Servo] He's trying things in this
movie he later perfected in Red Zone Cuba.

824
00:37:45,632 --> 00:37:49,368
[gunshots]

825
00:37:49,435 --> 00:37:52,270
-Stay alive, whatever Kurator find.

826
00:37:53,205 --> 00:37:54,473
[gunshots]

827
00:37:54,540 --> 00:37:57,577
-[Mike] Hold on. Just let me
clear out here. I'll just--

828
00:37:58,110 --> 00:38:00,278
-Uh-oh. This darn gun.

829
00:38:03,948 --> 00:38:05,417
Just a sec, people!

830
00:38:05,952 --> 00:38:07,853
-[Servo] Tor be late for interview.

831
00:38:08,953 --> 00:38:10,422
-[Mike] Better call the Kremlin.

832
00:38:11,123 --> 00:38:14,093
-Just don't shoot while I'm reloading!
That wouldn't be fair! OK, OK.

833
00:38:14,160 --> 00:38:16,795
[gunshots]

834
00:38:17,730 --> 00:38:20,498
-[Servo] Man desert horse no name.

835
00:38:20,565 --> 00:38:23,035
[humming]

836
00:38:23,968 --> 00:38:25,003
[gunshot]

837
00:38:26,072 --> 00:38:27,673
-[Mike] Apparently I'm dead.
Ugh.

838
00:38:30,275 --> 00:38:32,310
-Wonder if wife pack lunch.

839
00:38:33,778 --> 00:38:35,613
-[Servo] I'll just hail a cab
and get out of here.

840
00:38:38,517 --> 00:38:39,685
-[Mike] We'll never find him.

841
00:38:39,752 --> 00:38:41,920
Tor just blends right into the foliage.

842
00:38:44,590 --> 00:38:47,393
-Tor was a Navy Seal,
so he can handle this.

843
00:38:48,293 --> 00:38:51,563
-[Servo] No, no, Mrs. Kruschev
has wandered off again.

844
00:38:53,365 --> 00:38:56,268
-[Mike] You know, I thought they
didn't look like Yucca Flatians.

845
00:38:57,837 --> 00:39:00,973
-[Crow in Russian accent] So,
you hear that new Yakov Smirnoff album?

846
00:39:05,077 --> 00:39:07,178
[narrator] Yucca Flats.
-[Servo] I'll say.

847
00:39:07,613 --> 00:39:09,382
[narrator] The A-bomb.
-[Mike] And thou.

848
00:39:12,918 --> 00:39:14,953
-You know, Tor is much like
the Thompson's gazelle.

849
00:39:15,020 --> 00:39:19,292
You know, running, leaping
to elude his predators, and, uh...

850
00:39:20,025 --> 00:39:21,260
[explosion]

851
00:39:21,327 --> 00:39:23,195
-[Servo] Oh, that's what
that button does.

852
00:39:23,262 --> 00:39:26,198
-[Mike] Should we have warned
the nearby town? Nah.

853
00:39:26,265 --> 00:39:27,700
-My Lunchables!

854
00:39:30,568 --> 00:39:33,072
-[Servo] You dare question the great Oz?

855
00:39:42,715 --> 00:39:45,583
-[Servo] Coleman Francis had
a dark, muddy vision.

856
00:39:46,018 --> 00:39:47,152
With some cars.

857
00:39:49,922 --> 00:39:52,290
Yep. Well...

858
00:39:52,357 --> 00:39:53,758
-[Crow] Hm.
-[Servo] Come on.

859
00:39:53,825 --> 00:39:55,293
-[Mike] Get on over here, now.

860
00:39:56,828 --> 00:39:59,132
You know, parallel parking
is easy in Nevada.

861
00:39:59,198 --> 00:40:00,800
You gotta say that for it.

862
00:40:02,167 --> 00:40:05,905
-We could really use another
A-bomb explosion right about now.

863
00:40:07,640 --> 00:40:09,775
-[Servo] See Bonnie and Clyde's death
car!

864
00:40:11,810 --> 00:40:13,512
-[Mike] It was a dark and boring night.

865
00:40:20,052 --> 00:40:21,820
-Carl Sandberg?

866
00:40:21,887 --> 00:40:23,488
[narrator] Vacation time.

867
00:40:23,555 --> 00:40:24,923
Man and wife.

868
00:40:25,690 --> 00:40:27,560
Unaware of scientific progress.

869
00:40:27,627 --> 00:40:30,128
-[Servo] But they know they
need a new dishwasher.

870
00:40:33,198 --> 00:40:35,600
-I told you kids to
settle down back here!

871
00:40:35,835 --> 00:40:38,303
-Oh, wait.
The engine's in the front.

872
00:40:39,272 --> 00:40:41,840
-[Servo] I never said that!
It's so funny you think I would say that.

873
00:40:41,907 --> 00:40:43,342
I never said that.

874
00:40:44,143 --> 00:40:45,177
-[Mike] Wait, don't tell me.

875
00:40:45,243 --> 00:40:48,547
Tor Johnson, you old
son of a so and so!

876
00:40:50,048 --> 00:40:52,450
-I wonder if I look like Laura Dern.

877
00:40:52,518 --> 00:40:53,618
Nah.

878
00:40:53,685 --> 00:40:54,953
No.

879
00:40:55,020 --> 00:40:56,188
No.

880
00:40:56,655 --> 00:40:58,457
-[Servo] OK, OK, I'm dead already.

881
00:40:58,523 --> 00:40:59,725
Jeez.

882
00:41:01,493 --> 00:41:04,830
-[Mike] Honey,
we have to be to Izatys by 4:00.

883
00:41:06,432 --> 00:41:08,900
-He's going to use him as a car jack.

884
00:41:10,635 --> 00:41:12,805
-[Servo] There was no such thing
as clinical depression

885
00:41:12,872 --> 00:41:14,005
until this film was made.

886
00:41:17,342 --> 00:41:19,512
-[Mike] This is a wonderful honeymoon.

887
00:41:25,383 --> 00:41:27,285
-[Servo] Now how'd he get in the back?

888
00:41:28,653 --> 00:41:31,157
-Mom, he's touching me!

889
00:41:32,558 --> 00:41:37,162
-[Mike] Oh, Tor went to some, uh, fancy
club last night and got his hand stamped.

890
00:41:37,228 --> 00:41:39,698
-[Servo] Kenneth Branagh's
Mary Shelley's Bram Stoker's

891
00:41:39,765 --> 00:41:42,400
Wes Craven's Tim Burton's
Beast of Yucca Flats,

892
00:41:42,467 --> 00:41:44,102
a Francis Ford Coppola film.

893
00:41:46,872 --> 00:41:48,973
-[Mike] I'll just sleep till we get
there.

894
00:41:50,108 --> 00:41:52,545
-[Servo] Some marriages
just get off to a rocky start.

895
00:41:55,013 --> 00:41:58,883
-This will not help the
Discover Yucca Flats campaign.

896
00:42:00,318 --> 00:42:03,055
-[Servo humming along with eerie music]

897
00:42:03,122 --> 00:42:06,592
-[Mike] Honey, we at grandma's.

898
00:42:07,693 --> 00:42:10,228
-[Servo] My turn to drive.
I'll just climb over you.

899
00:42:11,630 --> 00:42:14,400
-[Crow] Yep. Look like you need
new alternator light.

900
00:42:17,235 --> 00:42:20,105
-[Mike] Tor went bobbing
for rubber cement again.

901
00:42:20,973 --> 00:42:23,708
-[Servo] Oh, Tor pull huge groin muscle.
Ow.

902
00:42:28,113 --> 00:42:31,750
[narrator] Joseph Javorsky,
noted scientist.

903
00:42:31,817 --> 00:42:33,918
-[Mike] "Melted scientist"?

904
00:42:34,253 --> 00:42:36,988
[narrator] Dedicated his life
to the betterment of mankind.

905
00:42:37,055 --> 00:42:38,457
-And girlkind.

906
00:42:41,893 --> 00:42:44,162
-[Servo] He always did want
a great pair of legs.

907
00:42:46,432 --> 00:42:49,768
-[Mike] You can see why Georgia O'Keefe
loved this part of the country.

908
00:42:50,202 --> 00:42:53,672
-The choreography leaves
something to be desired.

909
00:42:53,738 --> 00:42:57,108
-[Servo] Must get home to catch
Five Mrs. Buchanans.

910
00:42:59,077 --> 00:43:01,713
-[Mike] Never use yourself
to block your tires.

911
00:43:01,780 --> 00:43:02,948
-[Servo] No.

912
00:43:03,715 --> 00:43:05,517
-Well, here's your problem.

913
00:43:05,583 --> 00:43:06,785
You're dead!

914
00:43:08,087 --> 00:43:09,722
-[Servo] I'm Cherokee Jack.

915
00:43:13,658 --> 00:43:15,127
-[Mike] Well, I'm sure he's fine.

916
00:43:15,193 --> 00:43:16,495
I'm sure it's nothing.

917
00:43:18,030 --> 00:43:19,898
-[Crow] I, uh, gotta go tell my mom.

918
00:43:22,635 --> 00:43:24,837
-[Servo] Think, dammit, think!
Must get a car!

919
00:43:24,903 --> 00:43:26,272
Oh.

920
00:43:27,005 --> 00:43:30,308
-[Mike] He leaves to the strains of
the Cherokee Jack theme.

921
00:43:30,375 --> 00:43:31,777
-[Servo humming]

922
00:43:31,843 --> 00:43:33,745
-[Mike] ♪ Cherokee, Cherokee Jack ♪

923
00:43:33,812 --> 00:43:35,247
-[Servo] ♪ Cherokee, Cherokee ♪

924
00:43:35,313 --> 00:43:38,117
-Oh boy. Another great car
approaching scene.

925
00:43:38,183 --> 00:43:41,853
-[Servo] How do you decide when to turn
on your lights in a Coleman Francis movie?

926
00:43:43,022 --> 00:43:45,357
-[Mike] The Bartok music
really makes the scene.

927
00:43:45,423 --> 00:43:47,625
-[Servo] ♪ Cherokee, Cherokee... ♪
[humming]

928
00:43:47,693 --> 00:43:49,662
-[Crow] You know,
this could be Coleman Francis's

929
00:43:49,728 --> 00:43:52,263
very best car approaching sequence ever.

930
00:43:52,330 --> 00:43:53,765
-[Mike] No.
-[Crow] Yes.

931
00:43:53,832 --> 00:43:55,767
-[Servo]
♪ I'll have a cup of coffee and a roll ♪

932
00:43:55,833 --> 00:43:57,602
♪ And maybe just a roll
And maybe then some crullers ♪

933
00:43:57,670 --> 00:43:58,937
♪ And a danish, one or two ♪

934
00:43:59,003 --> 00:44:00,405
[narrator] Young Joe Dobson.

935
00:44:01,573 --> 00:44:02,742
Desert patrolman.

936
00:44:02,808 --> 00:44:04,877
-[Mike] Lover of fine wines.

937
00:44:04,943 --> 00:44:06,678
-[Servo] Finder of lost gloves.

938
00:44:07,412 --> 00:44:09,382
[honking]

939
00:44:13,352 --> 00:44:15,120
-Officer.
-Yeah?

940
00:44:15,187 --> 00:44:18,157
-There's a man dead,
back down the road.

941
00:44:18,223 --> 00:44:19,323
-Dead?

942
00:44:19,392 --> 00:44:21,360
-How far down?
-Couple miles.

943
00:44:21,427 --> 00:44:22,895
Maybe more.

944
00:44:23,428 --> 00:44:25,063
Laying behind his car.

945
00:44:25,497 --> 00:44:26,932
Looks like he's been choked.

946
00:44:26,998 --> 00:44:28,900
-Real actual dialogue.

947
00:44:31,737 --> 00:44:36,142
-[Mike] Fine, I'll just put off breakfast
and investigate your little murder.

948
00:44:36,208 --> 00:44:39,412
[Servo] Oh, no, I can't have a cruller.
I have to look at a body.

949
00:44:39,478 --> 00:44:42,948
-[Mike] Why do I have to go now?
He'll be just as dead after breakfast.

950
00:44:49,722 --> 00:44:51,490
-[Mike] Wow, he's dead.

951
00:44:55,393 --> 00:44:57,997
-[Servo] Oh, it's Westworld.

952
00:45:01,233 --> 00:45:02,567
-OK, car.

953
00:45:02,633 --> 00:45:03,902
Draw.

954
00:45:03,968 --> 00:45:05,403
Oh.

955
00:45:05,470 --> 00:45:09,942
-[Mike] What the hell?
It's got a Nash front and a VW back.

956
00:45:10,475 --> 00:45:13,445
-[Servo] Good thing I came around.
Battery could've died.

957
00:45:17,715 --> 00:45:18,883
-Oh, my God.

958
00:45:18,950 --> 00:45:22,855
In all my years on the force,
I've never seen a flat tire like that.

959
00:45:24,388 --> 00:45:27,192
-[Mike] Sir, do you have any idea
how fast you were dying?

960
00:45:30,362 --> 00:45:31,397
[narrator] Joe Dobson.

961
00:45:31,463 --> 00:45:32,932
-[Servo] Bitter over missing breakfast.

962
00:45:32,998 --> 00:45:35,000
[narrator]
Caught in the wheels of progress.

963
00:45:35,100 --> 00:45:38,403
-[Mike] Having trouble logging on
to the information superhighway.

964
00:45:42,340 --> 00:45:45,343
-I'm still trying to figure out
that "flag on the moon" thing.

965
00:45:45,410 --> 00:45:46,978
-[Mike] I hear you.

966
00:45:47,045 --> 00:45:49,448
-[Servo] I'll have to ask the interior
to step out of the car, please.

967
00:45:51,617 --> 00:45:54,285
-[Mike] Hey, a little bit of melted
leftover Kit-Kat bar.

968
00:45:54,352 --> 00:45:56,087
[narrator] Man choked to death.

969
00:45:56,155 --> 00:45:58,657
-[Mike] On a purse.
-[narrator] A woman's purse.

970
00:45:59,625 --> 00:46:01,593
And footprints on the wasteland.

971
00:46:01,927 --> 00:46:03,862
-[Mike] His limericks aren't very good.

972
00:46:07,800 --> 00:46:09,000
-[Servo] Watch out for snakes!

973
00:46:14,607 --> 00:46:16,542
-[Mike] Nope, breakfast calls.

974
00:46:20,278 --> 00:46:21,813
-Fine, thanks.

975
00:46:21,880 --> 00:46:23,615
Just leave me here.

976
00:46:24,783 --> 00:46:27,252
-[Servo] Let's see now. Let's see.
Should I have French toast, or--

977
00:46:27,318 --> 00:46:28,853
No! The blockbuster breakfast.

978
00:46:28,920 --> 00:46:33,025
Yeah. Ooh, heaps of hash browns,
sweet, flaky pastries, some sausages.

979
00:46:33,092 --> 00:46:34,893
Yum, yum, yum, yum.

980
00:46:36,562 --> 00:46:39,532
[narrator] Touch a button, things
happen.

981
00:46:39,598 --> 00:46:40,598
-[Mike] Sometimes.

982
00:46:40,665 --> 00:46:43,402
[narrator] A scientist becomes a beast.

983
00:46:43,468 --> 00:46:46,372
-This beast stuff is
harder than I thought.

984
00:46:49,140 --> 00:46:51,910
[Servo] Art thou dead, little cutie?

985
00:46:51,977 --> 00:46:54,445
-[Mike] Hey, I could use a hand here.

986
00:46:55,647 --> 00:46:57,983
-It's now time to make move.

987
00:46:59,217 --> 00:47:00,218
-[Servo] Honey.

988
00:47:00,285 --> 00:47:03,588
Oh, honey, what I wouldn't
do for hair like that.

989
00:47:05,357 --> 00:47:07,192
-[Mike] Hey, Tor, got a match?

990
00:47:07,258 --> 00:47:09,695
-[robots and Mike laugh]
-I love that one.

991
00:47:16,602 --> 00:47:19,170
-Lunch is at 11:30, right, Mike?

992
00:47:19,270 --> 00:47:20,338
-Yeah.

993
00:47:20,405 --> 00:47:22,975
-So is it 11:30, Mike?

994
00:47:23,442 --> 00:47:24,475
-No.

995
00:47:28,613 --> 00:47:30,415
-Now is it 11:30?

996
00:47:31,082 --> 00:47:32,517
-No.

997
00:47:32,583 --> 00:47:35,220
-Well, how soon until 11:30 is it?

998
00:47:35,287 --> 00:47:36,988
-Oh, you don't want to know.

999
00:47:40,858 --> 00:47:42,727
[squeaking]

1000
00:47:47,365 --> 00:47:49,467
-It has to be 11:30 now.

1001
00:47:49,868 --> 00:47:52,603
-It is so not 11:30.

1002
00:47:52,938 --> 00:47:55,373
-It can't not be 11:30.

1003
00:47:55,440 --> 00:47:57,808
-It couldn't be less 11:30.

1004
00:47:58,310 --> 00:48:03,815
-[humming]

1005
00:48:06,685 --> 00:48:09,453
-Is it almost 11:30 now?

1006
00:48:09,855 --> 00:48:12,523
-It is so not 11:30.

1007
00:48:13,125 --> 00:48:16,060
-You can't tell me
it's not 11:30 yet.

1008
00:48:16,127 --> 00:48:19,932
-It has never been less
11:30 than it is right now.

1009
00:48:31,577 --> 00:48:33,245
-Is it 11:30, Mike?

1010
00:48:34,012 --> 00:48:36,047
-Ah, it's completely 11:30.

1011
00:48:36,113 --> 00:48:37,548
-Oh!

1012
00:48:38,950 --> 00:48:40,285
Ha-ha!

1013
00:48:40,952 --> 00:48:42,053
Mm, mm.

1014
00:48:42,120 --> 00:48:43,655
When will it be 11:30 again, Mike?

1015
00:48:43,722 --> 00:48:45,690
-Oh, well, we just went through the--
[movie sign buzzer]

1016
00:48:45,757 --> 00:48:47,725
-Oh, we got movie sign.
-What? What? What?

1017
00:48:47,892 --> 00:48:49,193
-Take your sandwich and come.

1018
00:49:01,673 --> 00:49:03,375
-[Servo] What were you
talking about there?

1019
00:49:03,442 --> 00:49:04,843
-[Mike] I don't remember.

1020
00:49:06,077 --> 00:49:09,948
-[Servo] ♪ Hosanna superstar ♪

1021
00:49:11,783 --> 00:49:14,285
-[Mike] Maybe these people
have some crullers.

1022
00:49:15,420 --> 00:49:17,588
-[Joe] Jim?
-[Crow] Oh?

1023
00:49:17,990 --> 00:49:19,992
-[Jim] Be down in a minute, Joe.

1024
00:49:20,058 --> 00:49:22,160
-She's wearing her Wonder-nightie.

1025
00:49:25,063 --> 00:49:27,098
-[Mike] She's an attractive man.

1026
00:49:27,165 --> 00:49:28,667
[Crow groans]

1027
00:49:30,068 --> 00:49:31,268
-[Servo] Ugh!

1028
00:49:31,370 --> 00:49:33,572
OK, breasts. We get it.

1029
00:49:34,372 --> 00:49:37,208
-[Joe] Yeah.
Better come with me.

1030
00:49:37,275 --> 00:49:39,543
Trouble up the road.
Murder.

1031
00:49:39,610 --> 00:49:40,712
-[Jim] Be right down.

1032
00:49:40,778 --> 00:49:43,115
-I'm glad I can talk to you so openly.

1033
00:49:45,083 --> 00:49:48,020
-[Mike] Oh, uh, flag on the moon, too,
by the way.

1034
00:49:50,355 --> 00:49:51,823
-[Servo] Say hello to your mom.

1035
00:49:54,025 --> 00:49:55,060
-That's a hard face.

1036
00:49:55,127 --> 00:49:57,562
That's a face that challenges you.
-[Servo] Yep.

1037
00:50:00,265 --> 00:50:01,933
-[Jim] See you later, honey.

1038
00:50:02,467 --> 00:50:06,270
-[Mike] Coleman Francis solves
the problem of sound sync.

1039
00:50:06,337 --> 00:50:08,707
[narrator] Jim Archer, Joe's partner.

1040
00:50:08,773 --> 00:50:09,775
-[Servo] Really should wear a shirt.

1041
00:50:09,842 --> 00:50:12,443
[narrator] Another man
caught in the frantic race

1042
00:50:12,510 --> 00:50:15,247
for the betterment of mankind.

1043
00:50:15,947 --> 00:50:17,282
Progress.

1044
00:50:17,815 --> 00:50:21,553
-[Crow] You know, your short window
peepers really hate this house.

1045
00:50:22,987 --> 00:50:24,388
-[Mike] Affordable tract housing.

1046
00:50:24,455 --> 00:50:27,025
-[Servo] That place probably has
an assumable mortgage.

1047
00:50:28,060 --> 00:50:29,728
[narrator] Jim Archer.
-Yo!

1048
00:50:29,795 --> 00:50:32,130
[narrator] Wounded parachuting on Korea.

1049
00:50:32,730 --> 00:50:36,300
Jim and Joe try to keep
the desert roads safe for travelers...

1050
00:50:37,502 --> 00:50:38,970
seven days a week.

1051
00:50:39,070 --> 00:50:41,707
-[Mike] They really wish they
could get some time off.

1052
00:50:44,142 --> 00:50:45,977
-Oh, there he is.
Sorry.

1053
00:50:50,315 --> 00:50:54,518
-[Servo] July 12th, Tor establishes
base camp at the foot of Mount Shasta.

1054
00:50:56,788 --> 00:50:58,890
-[Mike] Now carry over threshold.

1055
00:50:58,957 --> 00:51:01,192
[humming "Here Comes the Bride"]

1056
00:51:04,562 --> 00:51:08,400
-Jeez, a phrenologist would
have a field day with Tor's skull.

1057
00:51:09,500 --> 00:51:11,903
-[Servo] She's not that heavy, but
she's shaped kinda awkward.

1058
00:51:14,338 --> 00:51:16,975
-Here, you take the good boulders.

1059
00:51:19,978 --> 00:51:22,513
-[Servo] It's like
being abducted by Montana.

1060
00:51:22,980 --> 00:51:25,150
[narrator] Shock waves of an A-bomb.

1061
00:51:26,050 --> 00:51:28,753
A once powerful, humble man

1062
00:51:28,820 --> 00:51:31,623
reduced to... nothing.

1063
00:51:31,690 --> 00:51:33,292
-[Mike] But hey, he keeps busy.

1064
00:51:38,763 --> 00:51:42,933
-[Servo] So anyway, you can see
how this all adds up to a movie.

1065
00:51:48,507 --> 00:51:51,175
-I always cry
during these scenes.

1066
00:51:51,977 --> 00:51:54,578
-[Mike] Tor want to make it with you.

1067
00:51:56,380 --> 00:51:58,517
-[Servo] A-ha, Night on Butt Mountain.

1068
00:51:58,583 --> 00:52:00,952
-[Mike] Come up here where
it's really obscene!

1069
00:52:03,722 --> 00:52:06,057
-[Servo] Ricola!

1070
00:52:06,525 --> 00:52:07,692
-[Mike] Love that.

1071
00:52:09,828 --> 00:52:11,730
-[Servo] Tor snaps into action!

1072
00:52:14,065 --> 00:52:17,168
-Sorry, honey.
Hadn't realized you dead.

1073
00:52:19,070 --> 00:52:20,805
-[Servo] I'm gonna play nine holes,
sweetie.

1074
00:52:20,872 --> 00:52:22,307
I'll be back.

1075
00:52:27,878 --> 00:52:29,915
-[Mike] It's a total eclipse of the sun.

1076
00:52:32,683 --> 00:52:33,952
[narrator] Joseph Javorsky.

1077
00:52:34,018 --> 00:52:35,253
-[Crow] Rooty Patooty.

1078
00:52:35,320 --> 00:52:37,288
[narrator] Respected scientist.

1079
00:52:37,355 --> 00:52:39,123
Now a fiend.
-[Servo] But he's my fiend.

1080
00:52:39,190 --> 00:52:41,258
[narrator] Prowling the wastelands,

1081
00:52:41,660 --> 00:52:45,097
a prehistoric beast in the nuclear age.

1082
00:52:45,930 --> 00:52:47,265
Kill.

1083
00:52:47,765 --> 00:52:49,968
Kill just to be killing.

1084
00:52:50,268 --> 00:52:53,305
-[Mike] ♪ Hills alive
Sound of music ♪

1085
00:52:55,073 --> 00:52:59,443
Father Mackenzie, darning his socks
in the night when there's nobody there.

1086
00:53:01,012 --> 00:53:05,483
[narrator] The mouth of the cave,
1,000 feet up over jagged cliffs.

1087
00:53:06,450 --> 00:53:08,118
A man murdered.

1088
00:53:08,720 --> 00:53:10,422
A woman's purse.

1089
00:53:10,855 --> 00:53:13,458
-[Servo] A thin plot, endlessly
restated.

1090
00:53:16,995 --> 00:53:20,632
-[Mike] I think I left a bag of
crullers up here last month.

1091
00:53:21,398 --> 00:53:23,668
-[Servo] This is one tough par five.

1092
00:53:27,672 --> 00:53:31,108
[narrator] Jim and Joe pick their way
upward to the mouth of the cave.

1093
00:53:31,175 --> 00:53:33,010
-[Servo] To fetch a pail of water.

1094
00:53:33,077 --> 00:53:34,112
[narrator] One slip...

1095
00:53:34,178 --> 00:53:35,447
-[Servo] One pair of panties.

1096
00:53:35,513 --> 00:53:37,815
[narrator] ...and 1,000 feet to nowhere.

1097
00:53:37,882 --> 00:53:39,817
-[Mike] I'm sorry,
I meant two feet to nowhere.

1098
00:53:39,883 --> 00:53:41,118
What am I thinking?

1099
00:53:43,788 --> 00:53:46,557
-[Crow] Joe? J-Joe?
Joe, what are you doing to that rock?

1100
00:53:46,625 --> 00:53:48,125
Joe, do not do that.

1101
00:53:48,192 --> 00:53:49,193
Joe.
-[Servo] Buddy.

1102
00:53:51,462 --> 00:53:53,030
Really touchy.

1103
00:53:57,635 --> 00:54:01,138
-[Mike] They haven't gotten very far
with the Crazy Horse monument.

1104
00:54:02,340 --> 00:54:04,275
-[Servo] Hello, mister-- Mr. Johnson?

1105
00:54:04,342 --> 00:54:06,543
Say, hello, Tors, are you home?

1106
00:54:06,612 --> 00:54:09,580
[Mike snoring]

1107
00:54:11,515 --> 00:54:13,452
-[Joe] Well,
there's the owner of the purse.

1108
00:54:13,518 --> 00:54:15,687
-[Jim] Yeah. Let's take her down.

1109
00:54:15,753 --> 00:54:17,488
-[Mike] Book her.
-[Jim] May still be alive.

1110
00:54:17,555 --> 00:54:18,990
-[Servo] Go ahead, ask her.

1111
00:54:20,658 --> 00:54:22,293
-[Joe] Is she still breathing?

1112
00:54:23,160 --> 00:54:25,130
-[Jim] Careful with her arm, Joe.

1113
00:54:25,830 --> 00:54:28,433
-I told you we should have
brought that two wheeler.

1114
00:54:28,500 --> 00:54:30,635
[Servo grunting]

1115
00:54:30,702 --> 00:54:32,470
-[Mike] Well, if you're going
to stand on her shoulder,

1116
00:54:32,570 --> 00:54:34,838
we're not going to be able
to pick her up, now are we?

1117
00:54:35,240 --> 00:54:38,042
-[Servo] I'm glad Tor didn't cocoon her
with his massive spinnerets.

1118
00:54:40,612 --> 00:54:42,880
-Maybe they should leave a note for Tor.

1119
00:54:43,748 --> 00:54:46,217
-[Servo] Now would be a good time
for some phrases.

1120
00:54:46,283 --> 00:54:48,118
A woman's purse.

1121
00:54:48,185 --> 00:54:49,487
Flag on the moon.

1122
00:54:49,753 --> 00:54:51,522
A man murdered.

1123
00:54:51,588 --> 00:54:53,358
I'll check back in a moment.

1124
00:54:56,227 --> 00:54:59,830
-[Crow] I think this is the single
hardest item on the scavenger hunt.

1125
00:55:01,165 --> 00:55:03,402
-[Jim] Hey, Joe. Here, feel her pulse.

1126
00:55:03,468 --> 00:55:04,735
-[Servo] Isn't that weird?

1127
00:55:05,870 --> 00:55:08,673
-[Joe] Well, doctors can't help her.

1128
00:55:09,073 --> 00:55:11,375
Maybe angels, but not doctors.

1129
00:55:11,442 --> 00:55:13,845
-[Mike] That's kind of
a girly thing to say, Joe.

1130
00:55:14,545 --> 00:55:16,715
Now feel my pulse, Joe.

1131
00:55:17,482 --> 00:55:19,950
-[Servo] She's dead,
so we can just toss her down, right?

1132
00:55:23,253 --> 00:55:25,090
-Paper, mister?
-[Crow] Why, yes it is.

1133
00:55:25,157 --> 00:55:28,560
You're a bright boy. [laughing]

1134
00:55:28,627 --> 00:55:31,028
-[Mike] Did the beast get
mentioned in Winchell?

1135
00:55:32,197 --> 00:55:34,532
-[Servo] Don't give me the floor model,
son. Give me a real one.

1136
00:55:34,598 --> 00:55:37,902
-They don't even have a daily paper,
and they're putting out an extra?

1137
00:55:38,870 --> 00:55:41,807
-[Mike] Kid's gotta sell
all three copies by noon.

1138
00:55:45,043 --> 00:55:48,012
-[Servo] Meanwhile,
in bustling downtown district,

1139
00:55:48,078 --> 00:55:49,947
or "the loop", as they call it.

1140
00:55:50,448 --> 00:55:52,883
-[Mike] Oh, good.
More driving action.

1141
00:55:57,122 --> 00:55:59,323
[narrator] Vacation time.
-So good bye.

1142
00:55:59,390 --> 00:56:03,895
[narrator] People travel east,
west, north, or south.

1143
00:56:03,962 --> 00:56:06,932
-[Servo] Some people just burrow
straight down, I guess.

1144
00:56:06,998 --> 00:56:09,467
[narrator] With two small boys.

1145
00:56:10,033 --> 00:56:11,668
Adventurous boys.

1146
00:56:11,702 --> 00:56:14,372
-[Mike] Boys who swordfight
in the desert sand.

1147
00:56:14,438 --> 00:56:16,742
[narrator] Nothing bothers some people.

1148
00:56:16,808 --> 00:56:18,375
Not even flying saucers.

1149
00:56:18,443 --> 00:56:19,577
-[Crow] What the-- huh?

1150
00:56:20,612 --> 00:56:22,780
-[Mike] I forgot what I came here for.

1151
00:56:24,382 --> 00:56:26,885
-Come on, Art.
Let's get some soda pop.

1152
00:56:27,452 --> 00:56:28,953
-[Servo] Check their oil, Lassie.

1153
00:56:29,020 --> 00:56:31,222
-[man] Hey, mister?
-[Mike] Can I have another doggie chew?

1154
00:56:31,288 --> 00:56:33,357
-[man] Uh, do you mind if we
bother you a minute?

1155
00:56:34,958 --> 00:56:37,895
-Well, as long as I don't
have to speak on camera.

1156
00:56:37,962 --> 00:56:39,697
[Mike] Here you go, doggie.

1157
00:56:40,498 --> 00:56:42,500
[grunting]

1158
00:56:42,567 --> 00:56:44,068
-[Servo] Tales of the gold monkey.

1159
00:56:45,203 --> 00:56:46,637
-[Mike] It's Tetanus Park!

1160
00:56:46,703 --> 00:56:48,172
[narrator] Boys from the city,

1161
00:56:48,673 --> 00:56:51,943
not yet caught
in the whirlwind of progress,

1162
00:56:52,810 --> 00:56:55,247
feed soda pop to the thirsty pigs.

1163
00:56:55,313 --> 00:56:57,315
-Pigs go better with Coke.

1164
00:56:58,315 --> 00:57:00,318
-[Servo] Pigs begin writing
on the side of the barn.

1165
00:57:03,220 --> 00:57:04,555
[narrator] Coyotes.

1166
00:57:04,988 --> 00:57:07,692
Once a menace to travelers.

1167
00:57:08,325 --> 00:57:10,828
Missile bases run them
off their hunting ground.

1168
00:57:10,895 --> 00:57:12,797
-That's another movie I'm working on.

1169
00:57:12,863 --> 00:57:13,932
-[Servo] Humphrey!

1170
00:57:15,400 --> 00:57:16,700
-How much do I owe you?

1171
00:57:16,767 --> 00:57:18,570
-$3.00.

1172
00:57:18,870 --> 00:57:21,172
-[woman] Boys!
We're leaving!

1173
00:57:21,238 --> 00:57:24,175
-[Mike] We're going to the ear wax
museum.

1174
00:57:24,242 --> 00:57:26,010
-You shouldn't have killed that pig.

1175
00:57:26,077 --> 00:57:27,178
-[Servo whispers] Sorry.

1176
00:57:28,913 --> 00:57:31,015
-Mom, we saw some real pigs.

1177
00:57:31,315 --> 00:57:32,817
-Yeah, and a coyote.

1178
00:57:32,883 --> 00:57:34,652
-Coyote?
-Yeah.

1179
00:57:34,718 --> 00:57:36,720
-Don't you be playing with coyotes.

1180
00:57:36,787 --> 00:57:39,723
Now come on.
We're leaving. Come on.

1181
00:57:39,790 --> 00:57:42,160
-Aw, we never get to have any fun.

1182
00:57:42,227 --> 00:57:44,528
-[Mike] But you looked at pigs.
And there's a tire.

1183
00:57:44,595 --> 00:57:46,063
You've had lots of fun.

1184
00:57:49,000 --> 00:57:51,435
-[Servo] So long, folks.
Enjoy our bleak landscape.

1185
00:57:58,677 --> 00:58:00,845
-How'd she get away from us?
She's dead.

1186
00:58:00,912 --> 00:58:03,715
-Well, she just gave me the slip, Joe.

1187
00:58:04,648 --> 00:58:07,018
[narrator] 110 in the shade...

1188
00:58:07,085 --> 00:58:09,787
-[Servo] 130 in my pants.
[narrator] ...and no shade.

1189
00:58:10,087 --> 00:58:13,290
Jim and Joe try to make
their way up to the plateau.

1190
00:58:14,158 --> 00:58:17,162
To reach the top,
a man needs an airplane.

1191
00:58:17,295 --> 00:58:18,597
-Or a big pogo stick.

1192
00:58:18,663 --> 00:58:21,967
[narrator] A jump from a plane
could land you on top.

1193
00:58:23,133 --> 00:58:24,602
-[Mike] Or a helicopter might do.

1194
00:58:24,668 --> 00:58:27,072
Did I say "flag on the moon" yet?

1195
00:58:27,805 --> 00:58:29,673
-[Servo] Why do they call this "flats"?

1196
00:58:29,740 --> 00:58:32,410
-I get the yucca part, but--

1197
00:58:32,477 --> 00:58:35,647
-[Servo] Oh, this is not good
for my groin injury! Ow.

1198
00:58:36,647 --> 00:58:38,215
-[Mike] Thank you for holding me, Ray.

1199
00:58:38,282 --> 00:58:39,783
You have soft hands.

1200
00:58:42,653 --> 00:58:45,323
Jeez, his star is the size
of a dinner plate.

1201
00:58:45,390 --> 00:58:46,390
[robots laugh]

1202
00:58:49,160 --> 00:58:52,830
-The beast put $100 down
and bought an old Studebaker.

1203
00:58:54,132 --> 00:58:57,368
-[Servo] Yucca Flats Frontier Days
is every August, by the way.

1204
00:58:57,435 --> 00:59:00,003
And the Yucca Flats Jaycees
are very active.

1205
00:59:02,573 --> 00:59:05,343
-[Mike] Maybe it's some other folks
caught up in the progress.

1206
00:59:05,410 --> 00:59:08,312
[narrator] But the killer's
not on the plateau.

1207
00:59:08,378 --> 00:59:11,482
-The killer's in Memphis,
marrying his 12-year-old cousin.

1208
00:59:14,252 --> 00:59:16,553
-[Servo] Javorsky. Ron Javorsky.

1209
00:59:16,620 --> 00:59:18,690
Played quarterback for the Eagles.

1210
00:59:20,223 --> 00:59:21,927
-[Mike] Well, I don't know
what we were doing

1211
00:59:21,993 --> 00:59:25,028
or why we climbed those rocks,
but here we are.

1212
00:59:26,097 --> 00:59:28,933
[narrator] Hours in the broiling hot
desert sun,

1213
00:59:29,667 --> 00:59:31,135
with no trace of the killer.

1214
00:59:31,202 --> 00:59:32,503
-Phooey.

1215
00:59:32,570 --> 00:59:37,442
[narrator] To put Jim Archer's paratrooper
training to good use is the only answer.

1216
00:59:37,508 --> 00:59:38,777
-[Servo] Here, have a little propane.

1217
00:59:38,843 --> 00:59:41,912
[narrator] A trip up into the skies
and jump.

1218
00:59:41,980 --> 00:59:43,280
-[Mike] Start flapping your arms.

1219
00:59:43,347 --> 00:59:47,618
[narrator] And if the killer
is on the plateau, kill him.

1220
00:59:47,685 --> 00:59:51,122
-Plus, Coleman has lots
of skydiving footage.

1221
00:59:52,690 --> 00:59:55,493
-[Servo] Come on, faster! Faster!
We're not in the air yet!

1222
01:00:00,263 --> 01:00:01,865
-[tire pops loudly]
-[Crow] Whoa!

1223
01:00:01,932 --> 01:00:03,935
A sudden attack of drunkenness sets in!

1224
01:00:07,205 --> 01:00:09,273
-[Mike] Right into your living room.
-[Servo] Whoa.

1225
01:00:12,677 --> 01:00:15,780
-[Servo] We invite you to visit
Yucca Flats for the beautiful fall colors.

1226
01:00:16,147 --> 01:00:17,782
-Hey, how'd they get in Cuba?

1227
01:00:20,618 --> 01:00:23,520
-[Mike] I'm going put
my little star on here. This could be--

1228
01:00:27,725 --> 01:00:30,795
-[Servo] Ah! That must be the Yucca
Flats.

1229
01:00:30,862 --> 01:00:31,962
-[laughs]
-[Mike groans]

1230
01:00:32,030 --> 01:00:33,363
-[Crow groans]

1231
01:00:33,430 --> 01:00:34,632
-[woman] Hank?

1232
01:00:34,698 --> 01:00:36,800
I don't see the boys.

1233
01:00:37,402 --> 01:00:38,670
-[Hank] I'll take a look.

1234
01:00:38,703 --> 01:00:40,672
-[Mike] Well,
they're not in the tire, honey.

1235
01:00:43,607 --> 01:00:46,343
-That is why the close-up was invented.

1236
01:00:48,913 --> 01:00:49,913
-[Servo] Sheriff Tintin.

1237
01:00:50,482 --> 01:00:52,617
-Jim, shoot first.

1238
01:00:53,083 --> 01:00:54,618
Ask questions later.

1239
01:00:54,685 --> 01:00:57,055
-[Mike] They're just little boys
who wandered off!

1240
01:01:00,992 --> 01:01:03,260
-The camera loves me.

1241
01:01:04,362 --> 01:01:06,830
-[Servo] The pubescentest gun in the
West.

1242
01:01:13,203 --> 01:01:14,672
-[Mike] Good luck, boingy.

1243
01:01:18,675 --> 01:01:21,778
-♪ Those mediocre men
In their flying machines ♪

1244
01:01:21,845 --> 01:01:23,247
-[Servo] ♪ They sort of go up ♪

1245
01:01:23,313 --> 01:01:25,550
♪ They kind of go uppity up, up ♪

1246
01:01:25,617 --> 01:01:26,750
-There you go.

1247
01:01:30,922 --> 01:01:32,690
[Mike hums to soundtrack]

1248
01:01:32,757 --> 01:01:35,192
♪ Yucca Flats ♪

1249
01:01:35,258 --> 01:01:37,595
♪ Yucca Flats ♪

1250
01:01:37,662 --> 01:01:40,965
-[Crow] Hank, I can't find me now.

1251
01:01:41,665 --> 01:01:45,368
-[Servo] A very nice prairie dog
taught me about being a woman.

1252
01:01:46,070 --> 01:01:48,305
-[Mike] What was I looking for?
-Hank?

1253
01:01:48,372 --> 01:01:49,773
I can't find them.

1254
01:01:50,775 --> 01:01:52,443
What do you think we should do?

1255
01:01:52,510 --> 01:01:55,012
-I don't know, Lois.
-[Mike] I'm not Hank. Does that help?

1256
01:01:55,078 --> 01:01:56,647
-Stay here. I'll go out farther.

1257
01:01:56,980 --> 01:01:58,182
We'll find them.

1258
01:01:59,083 --> 01:02:01,852
-Worrying about the kids
helps keep intimacy at bay.

1259
01:02:05,457 --> 01:02:07,325
-[Servo] Ah, we're up in the air!
Oh!

1260
01:02:09,927 --> 01:02:12,430
-Thank God for the Steadicam, huh?

1261
01:02:14,765 --> 01:02:16,200
-[Mike] Nope, no reaction.

1262
01:02:16,267 --> 01:02:17,735
Doesn't do it for me. Nope.

1263
01:02:21,805 --> 01:02:23,908
-[Servo] I love an old gold man.

1264
01:02:28,378 --> 01:02:29,713
-[Lois] Randy?

1265
01:02:30,347 --> 01:02:31,348
Art?

1266
01:02:31,415 --> 01:02:34,052
-The dingos took my baby.

1267
01:02:34,652 --> 01:02:38,255
-[Servo] ♪ I feel pretty
Oh so pretty ♪

1268
01:02:41,292 --> 01:02:42,560
-[Crow] Coleman gets arty!

1269
01:02:43,127 --> 01:02:46,663
-[Mike] You know, these two
do make a crack rescue team.

1270
01:02:49,233 --> 01:02:51,702
-[Servo] Wow! We could pee anywhere.

1271
01:02:51,768 --> 01:02:54,205
-Ron and Clint Howard in True West.

1272
01:02:55,138 --> 01:02:57,408
-[Mike] Sorry,
I still can't feel anything.

1273
01:02:57,475 --> 01:02:59,143
Nope. Nothing.

1274
01:03:03,982 --> 01:03:06,950
-[Servo] Jeez, the first
McDonald's Playlands weren't that good.

1275
01:03:08,920 --> 01:03:11,322
-What are they staring at?
Have they seen Christ?

1276
01:03:14,125 --> 01:03:16,827
-[Mike] I thought Shrubworld
would be more fun.

1277
01:03:21,998 --> 01:03:25,135
-Unstoppable parking action!
Whoo!

1278
01:03:25,202 --> 01:03:27,738
-[Servo] Marvel as they
get out of the car.

1279
01:03:29,173 --> 01:03:32,543
-[Servo]
♪ There's a bright golden haze on the-- ♪

1280
01:03:47,057 --> 01:03:48,325
-Art?

1281
01:03:49,060 --> 01:03:50,093
Randy?

1282
01:03:50,160 --> 01:03:52,028
-I'm feeling randy!

1283
01:03:53,163 --> 01:03:54,398
-[Servo] You Randy?

1284
01:03:54,465 --> 01:03:55,900
Nah.

1285
01:03:55,967 --> 01:03:57,502
-[Mike] Ah, hell, I put in enough time.

1286
01:03:59,203 --> 01:04:00,470
-Hey, a raccoon!

1287
01:04:08,212 --> 01:04:11,382
-[Mike] This whole sequence
can't go on long enough for me.

1288
01:04:14,685 --> 01:04:15,987
-[Servo] ♪ Hosanna ♪

1289
01:04:16,587 --> 01:04:21,158
♪ Hosanna superstar ♪

1290
01:04:21,658 --> 01:04:23,693
-[Mike whispers] Stop that.
-[Servo whispers] Sorry.

1291
01:04:23,760 --> 01:04:26,463
-It's Nickelodeon's Waiting for Godot.

1292
01:04:28,967 --> 01:04:31,702
-[Servo] What an oddly proportioned
little man, huh?

1293
01:04:34,072 --> 01:04:38,910
-[Crow] It was daring of Coleman to
change the theme from parking to walking.

1294
01:04:38,977 --> 01:04:40,310
-[Mike] The beast! Ah!

1295
01:04:40,377 --> 01:04:41,912
No.
-[Crow] Oh.

1296
01:04:41,978 --> 01:04:44,382
-[Servo] I'm a lunch lady at heart.

1297
01:04:45,783 --> 01:04:49,053
-I'm poor but clean,
downtrodden but proud.

1298
01:04:50,922 --> 01:04:53,890
-[Servo] This movie stops at nothing,
and stays there.

1299
01:04:54,692 --> 01:04:57,395
-[Mike] Well, at least my shirt
is quite comfortable.

1300
01:05:00,632 --> 01:05:02,767
-Nuclear test site
sure is well guarded.

1301
01:05:02,833 --> 01:05:05,670
-[Servo] It's like he got lost on
his way to the drugstore.

1302
01:05:06,003 --> 01:05:07,103
Huh?

1303
01:05:07,170 --> 01:05:08,505
Hm?

1304
01:05:08,572 --> 01:05:11,308
-[Mike] "Keep... oat.

1305
01:05:11,375 --> 01:05:12,377
Out?

1306
01:05:12,443 --> 01:05:15,613
Go verne mine-t pro-part."

1307
01:05:19,550 --> 01:05:21,085
-Well, on the good side,

1308
01:05:21,152 --> 01:05:23,187
this is time away from Mrs. Potatohead.

1309
01:05:26,757 --> 01:05:30,728
-[Mike] Art? I'm gonna have to make
a lean-to out of your hide.

1310
01:05:31,762 --> 01:05:35,065
-[Servo] Disappointing turnout
for REO Speedwagon at Alpine Valley.

1311
01:05:40,037 --> 01:05:42,607
-Shh. He's laying his eggs.

1312
01:05:44,208 --> 01:05:46,310
-[Mike] Why, it's Verne Gagne.

1313
01:05:47,545 --> 01:05:48,778
[narrator] Always on the prowl.

1314
01:05:48,845 --> 01:05:50,047
-[Servo] For the ladies.

1315
01:05:50,113 --> 01:05:52,983
[narrator] Looking for something
or somebody to kill.

1316
01:05:53,750 --> 01:05:55,720
Quench the killer's thirst.

1317
01:05:56,287 --> 01:05:59,157
-Well, we have mom's looks
and dad's intelligence.

1318
01:05:59,223 --> 01:06:01,058
We should be able to deal with this.

1319
01:06:02,460 --> 01:06:03,960
-[Mike] He looks like a lawn jockey.

1320
01:06:04,028 --> 01:06:05,595
[robots laugh]

1321
01:06:07,798 --> 01:06:11,102
-[Servo] ♪ One singular sensation ♪

1322
01:06:11,168 --> 01:06:13,037
[humming]

1323
01:06:17,307 --> 01:06:18,308
-[Servo] Huh?

1324
01:06:18,375 --> 01:06:20,143
-Tor, you've lost weight!

1325
01:06:24,882 --> 01:06:27,083
-[Mike] This guy is stealing this film.

1326
01:06:27,485 --> 01:06:29,553
-[Servo] Yeah, still can't get worked
up.

1327
01:06:33,390 --> 01:06:35,893
-Nope, nothing over there
excites me either.

1328
01:06:40,063 --> 01:06:42,633
-[Mike] Hey, look!
They opened the new Prange Way.

1329
01:06:45,837 --> 01:06:48,205
[Servo grunting]

1330
01:06:49,473 --> 01:06:51,142
-[Mike] I see a renegade shrub.

1331
01:06:56,680 --> 01:06:58,348
-Hey! Hey, hey, hey!
-[Servo] No no no.

1332
01:06:58,415 --> 01:06:59,450
[gunshot]

1333
01:07:01,452 --> 01:07:04,522
-[Mike] OK, ground, hold still.
-[bullet ricochets]

1334
01:07:04,588 --> 01:07:06,890
-[Servo] No,
don't shoot the dad of Yucca Flats!

1335
01:07:10,493 --> 01:07:13,597
-Vacationing at the Most
Dangerous Game Dude Ranch.

1336
01:07:17,033 --> 01:07:18,702
[gunshot]

1337
01:07:20,437 --> 01:07:22,440
-[Mike] Is that a Piper or a Beechcraft?

1338
01:07:22,807 --> 01:07:24,007
[gunshot]

1339
01:07:25,042 --> 01:07:28,045
-[Servo] This guy's a terrible shot.
No wonder we lost Korea.

1340
01:07:29,012 --> 01:07:30,547
[gunshot]

1341
01:07:30,613 --> 01:07:32,517
[laughter]

1342
01:07:32,583 --> 01:07:33,883
-Oh, owie, owie!

1343
01:07:35,987 --> 01:07:38,822
-[Mike] Oh, thought somebody
killed me there for a second.

1344
01:07:47,030 --> 01:07:48,198
[gunshot]

1345
01:07:48,465 --> 01:07:51,435
-[Servo] Whoa. Guess I'll have to dig
him out of the crack of Butt Mountain.

1346
01:07:51,502 --> 01:07:52,903
[bullets ricochet]

1347
01:07:52,970 --> 01:07:55,738
-What, have they got
a spider monkey holding the camera?

1348
01:07:57,608 --> 01:08:00,077
-[Mike] Feels kind of good.
Weird, but good.

1349
01:08:00,143 --> 01:08:01,645
[Servo laughing]

1350
01:08:04,615 --> 01:08:07,785
-Well, he's a spry little dad, isn't he?

1351
01:08:07,852 --> 01:08:09,720
-[Servo] Is this guy piloting
with his feet?

1352
01:08:09,787 --> 01:08:11,255
-[Crow] Ugh!

1353
01:08:11,322 --> 01:08:12,757
[gunshot]

1354
01:08:14,992 --> 01:08:17,260
-[Mike] Just holding the camera
in front of himself.

1355
01:08:21,032 --> 01:08:23,000
-Well, this doesn't do much for me.

1356
01:08:24,302 --> 01:08:26,503
-[Servo] Oh, he disguised himself
as a tumbleweed.

1357
01:08:26,570 --> 01:08:28,005
See?
-[gunshots]

1358
01:08:34,778 --> 01:08:38,582
-[Mike] At least I'm getting
a good aerobic workout.

1359
01:08:38,648 --> 01:08:41,518
[gunshots]
-[Servo] A bit of a rip-off.

1360
01:08:41,585 --> 01:08:43,487
-[Mike] Coleman steals from only the
best.

1361
01:08:46,857 --> 01:08:49,593
-Oh, this would happen to me!

1362
01:08:51,462 --> 01:08:54,665
-[Servo] Oh, ho-ho, that is hard
on the crotch, I must say.

1363
01:08:55,900 --> 01:08:58,268
-[Mike] Well, he was probably
guilty of something.

1364
01:08:59,637 --> 01:09:01,638
-Wanna get a good look
at that one pebble.

1365
01:09:01,705 --> 01:09:05,308
[narrator] Shoot first,
ask questions later.

1366
01:09:05,375 --> 01:09:08,078
-[Servo] Ask "Christian Slater"?
Okay...

1367
01:09:08,145 --> 01:09:11,682
-[Mike] Shoot the film first,
ask questions later.

1368
01:09:13,383 --> 01:09:15,318
-Great Waffle House down there!

1369
01:09:21,825 --> 01:09:23,593
-[Servo] Wha-- what? Wait, what did I
say?

1370
01:09:24,295 --> 01:09:26,630
-[Mike] ♪ Everything's going my-- ♪

1371
01:09:26,697 --> 01:09:28,098
Well, I'd better get back to--

1372
01:09:31,035 --> 01:09:32,570
[narrator] The pilot dropped his man.

1373
01:09:32,637 --> 01:09:34,003
-[Servo] Now he wants another one.

1374
01:09:34,070 --> 01:09:37,808
[narrator] If Joe Dobson moves north,
Hank will be caught in the middle.

1375
01:09:37,908 --> 01:09:39,510
-Of my constant droning.

1376
01:09:39,577 --> 01:09:42,980
[narrator] An innocent victim,
caught in the wheels of justice.

1377
01:09:43,047 --> 01:09:44,348
-[Mike] I've been handed a rewrite.

1378
01:09:44,415 --> 01:09:47,685
It's no longer "progress".
It's now "justice".

1379
01:09:51,955 --> 01:09:53,923
-[all singing to soundtrack]
♪ Francis ♪

1380
01:09:53,990 --> 01:09:59,897
♪ Coleman Francis
Coleman ♪

1381
01:10:01,198 --> 01:10:02,900
[narrator] A man runs.

1382
01:10:03,367 --> 01:10:04,702
Somebody shoots at him.

1383
01:10:04,768 --> 01:10:06,337
-[Servo] Sorry. That's all we got.

1384
01:10:06,403 --> 01:10:08,138
That's our movie.
Good bye.

1385
01:10:10,307 --> 01:10:12,475
-He's headed for Caesar's Palace.

1386
01:10:14,110 --> 01:10:16,547
-[Mike] Well, still, I'm not with my
wife.

1387
01:10:17,615 --> 01:10:19,215
[Servo] Ow.

1388
01:10:19,283 --> 01:10:21,252
This is not my beautiful gulch!

1389
01:10:22,318 --> 01:10:25,188
-[Crow] I thought they said June 5th!

1390
01:10:26,490 --> 01:10:28,625
-[Mike] The Shootist, with John Wayne.

1391
01:10:31,462 --> 01:10:33,297
-[Servo] Yep, he just landed, folks.

1392
01:10:38,302 --> 01:10:39,737
-What's he doing?

1393
01:10:39,803 --> 01:10:41,005
Oh! [laughs]

1394
01:10:41,072 --> 01:10:44,808
-[Mike] I really should say something
to him about shooting me.

1395
01:10:46,710 --> 01:10:47,778
[narrator] Jim Archer.

1396
01:10:47,845 --> 01:10:50,948
Ex-paratrooper,
trained to hunt down his man.

1397
01:10:51,015 --> 01:10:53,150
-[Servo purrs suggestively]
[narrator] And destroy him.

1398
01:10:53,217 --> 01:10:55,385
-[Mike] And your State Farm
representative.

1399
01:10:56,753 --> 01:10:59,490
-He wakes with a taste for heroin.

1400
01:10:59,557 --> 01:11:02,693
-[Servo] ♪ Hosana ♪

1401
01:11:02,760 --> 01:11:06,130
-[Servo] ♪ Hosanna superstar ♪

1402
01:11:06,197 --> 01:11:08,332
-[Mike] I told you about that.
-[Servo] Sorry.

1403
01:11:08,698 --> 01:11:11,268
-[Mike] Mm, dirt.
Two, three days old.

1404
01:11:13,170 --> 01:11:16,807
-I'm sick, I'm dying, and yet
I have a taste for nachos!

1405
01:11:16,907 --> 01:11:19,075
-[Mike] Mm, nachos.
That's what I need.

1406
01:11:19,142 --> 01:11:20,843
-[Servo] ♪ Hosanna ♪

1407
01:11:33,823 --> 01:11:34,858
-Hello.

1408
01:11:34,925 --> 01:11:36,927
I'm Crow T. Robot
of the Satellite of Love.

1409
01:11:36,993 --> 01:11:41,765
You know, The Beast of Yucca Flats,
Skydivers, and Red Zone Cuba

1410
01:11:41,832 --> 01:11:47,403
are just three examples of the many, many
godawful films made during this century.

1411
01:11:47,470 --> 01:11:52,142
Tragically, films like these
are not deteriorating fast enough.

1412
01:11:52,208 --> 01:11:55,345
That's why I urge you to support FAPS,

1413
01:11:55,412 --> 01:11:58,115
the Film Anti-Preservation Society.

1414
01:11:58,182 --> 01:12:02,418
At FAPS, we're devoted to allowing
the films of Coleman Francis

1415
01:12:02,485 --> 01:12:06,457
and countless others
to die a gentle, natural death.

1416
01:12:06,523 --> 01:12:09,827
We'll use your donations
to transfer these films

1417
01:12:09,893 --> 01:12:12,395
to fragile, volatile silver nitrate stock,

1418
01:12:12,462 --> 01:12:15,298
so they'll rot quickly
into nature's compost.

1419
01:12:15,365 --> 01:12:17,233
Now, here's how you can help.

1420
01:12:17,300 --> 01:12:21,538
If you find a copy of a film as bad as,
oh, Aspen Extreme,

1421
01:12:21,605 --> 01:12:25,042
please, store it in a warm,
moist, salty place

1422
01:12:25,108 --> 01:12:27,677
such as a cheese factory, or your mouth.

1423
01:12:27,745 --> 01:12:30,047
-Uh, Crow?
-The situation is serious.

1424
01:12:30,113 --> 01:12:31,782
If we don't take action now,

1425
01:12:31,848 --> 01:12:34,250
the entire filmography
of Sylvester Stallone

1426
01:12:34,317 --> 01:12:38,122
may be available for our children
and our grandchildren to view.

1427
01:12:38,188 --> 01:12:40,390
So if you want to save future generations

1428
01:12:40,457 --> 01:12:42,325
from the legacy of Cliffhanger,

1429
01:12:42,392 --> 01:12:44,395
Demolition Man, Cobra--
-Crow?

1430
01:12:44,462 --> 01:12:45,462
-Rhinestone.
-Crow?

1431
01:12:45,528 --> 01:12:47,697
-Oscar,
Stop, Or My Mom Will Shoot.

1432
01:12:47,765 --> 01:12:48,898
-Crow?

1433
01:12:48,965 --> 01:12:51,168
-I'm on television, Mike!
Nighthawks, Paradi--

1434
01:12:51,235 --> 01:12:54,070
-Uh, don't you think what
you're suggesting is wrong?

1435
01:12:55,072 --> 01:12:56,907
-You're right, Mike.
How awful of me.

1436
01:12:57,007 --> 01:12:58,208
Where was I?

1437
01:12:58,242 --> 01:13:00,710
So please, call now,
and pledge what you can.

1438
01:13:00,777 --> 01:13:03,513
Just dial 1-800-LETROT.

1439
01:13:03,580 --> 01:13:05,082
Won't you?
Thank you.

1440
01:13:05,148 --> 01:13:08,585
-[laughing] Yeah, Crow T. Robot
may or may not be back.

1441
01:13:08,652 --> 01:13:10,887
Uh, just come right this way.
Could you?

1442
01:13:10,953 --> 01:13:12,722
-Important film meeting?
-Yeah, that's right.

1443
01:13:12,790 --> 01:13:14,925
-I'm ready for my close up.
Mr. Katzenberg, hello!

1444
01:13:14,992 --> 01:13:16,127
-[Mike] You look great.

1445
01:13:25,135 --> 01:13:26,837
-[Mike] Love this movie.

1446
01:13:26,903 --> 01:13:30,107
-[Servo] Oh, boy.
I'm always surprised to see human feces.

1447
01:13:30,173 --> 01:13:32,075
[narrator] The hunter and the hunted.

1448
01:13:32,142 --> 01:13:33,877
-[Mike] This fall on ABC.

1449
01:13:33,910 --> 01:13:36,880
[narrator] With only a few hundred yards
between him and the enemy.

1450
01:13:36,947 --> 01:13:39,015
Jim closes in for the kill.

1451
01:13:39,082 --> 01:13:41,083
-[Servo] And just as Jim
closes in for the kill,

1452
01:13:41,152 --> 01:13:44,087
so does Mutual of Omaha
close in for the kill.

1453
01:13:44,153 --> 01:13:45,855
[Mike snickers]

1454
01:13:49,392 --> 01:13:53,363
-She was in Bergman film
where she played low self-esteem.

1455
01:13:53,730 --> 01:13:55,432
-[Mike] Today's Romanian woman.

1456
01:13:58,668 --> 01:14:00,337
-[Servo] Well, let's see.
Was I shot in the gut?

1457
01:14:00,403 --> 01:14:02,338
Or, no, the leg.
No, the arm, I think.

1458
01:14:02,405 --> 01:14:05,275
Well, some part of me hurts.
I'm not sure which. Ow.

1459
01:14:08,078 --> 01:14:11,382
-Harcourt Fenton Mudd,
you dirty, rotten, lazy thing,

1460
01:14:11,448 --> 01:14:13,150
thing, thing!

1461
01:14:13,717 --> 01:14:17,053
-[Mike] Bob Mathias is Chet Baker
in The Jesse Owens Story.

1462
01:14:17,888 --> 01:14:19,422
-[Servo] Oh, my muffins!
Oh my.

1463
01:14:22,458 --> 01:14:25,562
-Honey, I'm taking the kids
off the find list.

1464
01:14:25,628 --> 01:14:26,863
-[Lois] Where are the boys?

1465
01:14:26,930 --> 01:14:27,932
Why are you running?

1466
01:14:27,998 --> 01:14:30,633
-I have no time to explain, Lois.
Stay here.

1467
01:14:30,700 --> 01:14:32,002
The boys may come back.

1468
01:14:32,068 --> 01:14:33,103
I've gotta get help.

1469
01:14:33,170 --> 01:14:35,538
-Uh, but-- but-- but--
what-- don't leave!

1470
01:14:35,605 --> 01:14:38,608
I mean, c-can I have the canteen?

1471
01:14:38,908 --> 01:14:41,077
Oh, oh.

1472
01:14:41,110 --> 01:14:43,147
-[Mike] Lads, he is a wondrous mystery.

1473
01:14:45,982 --> 01:14:49,352
-Honey, my abandonment issues
are coming up!

1474
01:14:50,153 --> 01:14:52,588
[narrator] Joe Dobson headed north
and met Jim.

1475
01:14:52,655 --> 01:14:54,457
-[Mike] They married and settled down.

1476
01:14:56,092 --> 01:14:57,227
-[Servo] Well, gotta go!

1477
01:15:01,932 --> 01:15:04,067
-Hi. Shoot any joggers today?

1478
01:15:04,133 --> 01:15:06,637
-[Servo sings to soundtrack] ♪ Beast of
Yucca Flats, beast of, beast of ♪

1479
01:15:06,703 --> 01:15:08,072
♪ Beast of Yucca Flats ♪

1480
01:15:08,138 --> 01:15:11,308
[narrator] Twenty hours without rest
and still no enemy.

1481
01:15:11,375 --> 01:15:12,642
-[Mike] He has an S body.

1482
01:15:12,708 --> 01:15:14,377
[narrator] In the blistering desert
heat,

1483
01:15:14,410 --> 01:15:16,913
Jim and Joe plan another attack.

1484
01:15:16,980 --> 01:15:17,982
-[Servo] On what?

1485
01:15:18,048 --> 01:15:20,550
[narrator] Find the beast and kill him.

1486
01:15:20,617 --> 01:15:22,252
-OK, it's the same plan.

1487
01:15:22,318 --> 01:15:24,253
-[Servo purrs suggestively]
-[Mike] Ha-ha-ha.

1488
01:15:24,320 --> 01:15:27,357
[narrator] Man's inhumanity to man.

1489
01:15:27,423 --> 01:15:30,193
-[Servo] Or beast's inhubeastity to
beast.

1490
01:15:34,397 --> 01:15:36,267
-I'm blocking out the fact
that they shot me.

1491
01:15:36,333 --> 01:15:38,602
I'm not letting it get to me.

1492
01:15:40,270 --> 01:15:42,438
-[Servo] Oh, would you open the gate,
please?

1493
01:15:42,505 --> 01:15:44,742
I'm near death, for crying out--
Oh, jeez.

1494
01:15:50,013 --> 01:15:53,917
-[Mike] The fifth grade production
of Of Mice And Men.

1495
01:15:58,655 --> 01:16:01,090
[Servo hums to soundtrack]

1496
01:16:01,157 --> 01:16:02,392
-[Crow] Oh, there.

1497
01:16:02,458 --> 01:16:04,628
That feels a lot better.
I'm glad I did that.

1498
01:16:04,695 --> 01:16:06,497
Oh, no one-- no one will see that.

1499
01:16:06,563 --> 01:16:08,365
-[Servo] Heidi, the mature years.

1500
01:16:09,432 --> 01:16:10,700
-[Art] Randy?

1501
01:16:10,767 --> 01:16:12,368
You think we're lost?

1502
01:16:12,435 --> 01:16:14,805
-[Mike] You can't keep things from kids,
huh?

1503
01:16:14,872 --> 01:16:16,707
-[Randy] I don't know, Art.

1504
01:16:16,773 --> 01:16:18,608
Maybe. Let's go.

1505
01:16:18,675 --> 01:16:21,378
-[Crow] Those Donner party people
seem very nice.

1506
01:16:23,747 --> 01:16:24,782
Taxi?

1507
01:16:28,985 --> 01:16:32,855
-[Servo] Well, I suppose I'll just
sit here and wait for death to take me.

1508
01:16:34,223 --> 01:16:36,760
-[Mike] I'll sew.
That's what I'll do.

1509
01:16:38,862 --> 01:16:40,663
[Servo] Oh, honey.

1510
01:16:40,730 --> 01:16:43,833
-[Crow] Well, I'll permit myself
one Chiclet now.

1511
01:16:44,902 --> 01:16:48,672
-[Servo] I've been meaning to clean my
glasses for months, but you get so busy.

1512
01:16:49,572 --> 01:16:50,873
-[Mike] I'm sad.

1513
01:16:54,577 --> 01:16:56,513
-[Crow] Many years later.

1514
01:16:57,347 --> 01:16:59,683
-[Servo] So, did Francis promise you
back end, too?

1515
01:17:02,718 --> 01:17:06,390
-[Mike] Remarkably, the boys stumble
across the source of the Nile.

1516
01:17:09,258 --> 01:17:11,462
-[Crow] The concept of Wham! is born.

1517
01:17:12,928 --> 01:17:14,363
-[Art] Randy?

1518
01:17:14,430 --> 01:17:16,433
Is that water down there?

1519
01:17:16,500 --> 01:17:18,402
-[Randy] I sure hope so.

1520
01:17:18,468 --> 01:17:20,103
-[Art] My throat's dry.

1521
01:17:20,170 --> 01:17:21,505
-[Randy] Mine too.

1522
01:17:21,572 --> 01:17:24,908
-[Servo] I've seen water before,
and I'm pretty sure that's water.

1523
01:17:25,475 --> 01:17:27,410
-[Mike] He oughta pop that.
-[Servo] Ugh.

1524
01:17:29,947 --> 01:17:33,783
-[Crow] Should we worry about
the dead cows laying next to the pond?

1525
01:17:35,885 --> 01:17:38,088
-[Servo] Hi. Can I play through?

1526
01:17:39,355 --> 01:17:41,692
-[Mike] Those butts are ripe for
kicking.

1527
01:17:42,925 --> 01:17:45,195
-[Crow] Uh, well, it wasn't water.

1528
01:17:45,262 --> 01:17:46,997
-[Servo] Oh, cute crop top.

1529
01:17:51,233 --> 01:17:53,570
-[Mike] Well, we're in Wisconsin now.

1530
01:17:53,937 --> 01:17:57,307
Or maybe it's Cuba.
Or Nevada. Whatever.

1531
01:17:57,373 --> 01:17:59,910
-[howling]

1532
01:17:59,977 --> 01:18:02,445
-[Crow] Oh, the young Ed McMahon.

1533
01:18:03,813 --> 01:18:05,215
-[Servo] Cut it out, Mom.

1534
01:18:08,285 --> 01:18:11,388
-[Mike] I must pose
for my Dustbowl portrait.

1535
01:18:12,688 --> 01:18:17,027
-Back in '54, there were
many gentleman callers I had.

1536
01:18:17,393 --> 01:18:19,628
-[Mike] Where did they find
my third grade teacher?

1537
01:18:21,465 --> 01:18:24,067
-[Servo] I'm pretty sure this is a shot
intended to convey something.

1538
01:18:26,168 --> 01:18:28,538
-[Crow] This is an odd sort of stalking.

1539
01:18:30,807 --> 01:18:32,642
-[Mike] I swallowed a bug.

1540
01:18:32,708 --> 01:18:34,978
-[Crow] He must have dropped
a bowl of Cream of Wheat on his head

1541
01:18:35,012 --> 01:18:36,278
and it hardened.

1542
01:18:36,345 --> 01:18:37,513
[all chuckle]

1543
01:18:37,580 --> 01:18:40,250
-[Servo] I think Tor would be
more comfortable in a skirt, don't you?

1544
01:18:40,317 --> 01:18:41,552
-[Mike shudders]

1545
01:18:42,652 --> 01:18:44,855
Is that yellow tiger tail?

1546
01:18:44,922 --> 01:18:47,157
-[Crow] Oh, would you close your mouth?

1547
01:18:47,623 --> 01:18:49,558
-[growling]
-[Crow imitates growl]

1548
01:18:49,625 --> 01:18:51,227
-[growling]

1549
01:18:51,293 --> 01:18:55,098
-[Servo laughs] You know, for Tor,
sneaking up and pursuit are not his forte.

1550
01:18:58,168 --> 01:19:01,472
-[Mike] You know, Tor was originally
offered the role of the mom in this film.

1551
01:19:06,308 --> 01:19:08,878
[Servo beatboxing to soundtrack]

1552
01:19:09,780 --> 01:19:12,348
-[Servo] Oh, I think she's going
to do an impression.

1553
01:19:14,483 --> 01:19:16,653
-After a brief moment of almost action,

1554
01:19:16,720 --> 01:19:18,388
we're back to our normal pace.

1555
01:19:22,025 --> 01:19:24,727
-[Servo] Flag on the moon.
How'd it get there?

1556
01:19:24,795 --> 01:19:27,030
[whispers] What the hell does that mean?

1557
01:19:28,097 --> 01:19:32,568
-[Mike] You know, this place is probably
nicer than Javorsky's place in Moscow.

1558
01:19:32,635 --> 01:19:33,837
-[Art] Randy?

1559
01:19:33,903 --> 01:19:35,038
-[Randy] What?

1560
01:19:35,105 --> 01:19:36,473
-[Art] Where are we?

1561
01:19:36,540 --> 01:19:37,873
-[Randy] In a cave, I guess.

1562
01:19:37,940 --> 01:19:40,310
-[Servo scoffs]
-Come on, Art.

1563
01:19:40,377 --> 01:19:41,410
Be careful.

1564
01:19:41,478 --> 01:19:43,213
-[Mike] Randy?
Do we state the obvious?

1565
01:19:43,280 --> 01:19:46,448
-[Art] Think that old man
can get us up here?

1566
01:19:46,517 --> 01:19:49,085
-[Randy] Don't talk about it.
Come on.

1567
01:19:49,152 --> 01:19:52,822
-[Crow] You're blocking, Randy.
We have issues to deal with.

1568
01:19:53,723 --> 01:19:57,160
-[Mike] Mm-hm.
How the hell did I get up here?

1569
01:19:57,893 --> 01:20:01,932
-[Servo] ♪ High on hill
Live lonely goatherd, lay lay loo ♪

1570
01:20:01,998 --> 01:20:05,302
-[Crow] Tor is all the Brothers Johnson
rolled into one.

1571
01:20:08,172 --> 01:20:10,440
-[Randy] Art, get back.

1572
01:20:10,507 --> 01:20:13,177
-[Mike] Oh, false alarm. It's not Mom.

1573
01:20:14,510 --> 01:20:17,247
-[grunting]

1574
01:20:17,313 --> 01:20:21,283
-[Servo] Why?! Why can't I get
a baked potato before 5:00 in this town?

1575
01:20:22,218 --> 01:20:25,388
-[continues grunting]

1576
01:20:28,692 --> 01:20:30,760
-[Crow] They never should have
let Tor improv.

1577
01:20:30,827 --> 01:20:33,797
-[Servo and Mike imitate grunting]

1578
01:20:39,970 --> 01:20:43,038
[narrator] The beast,
finding his victim gone,

1579
01:20:43,105 --> 01:20:44,573
unleashes his fury.

1580
01:20:45,308 --> 01:20:47,443
-[Mike] Aw, fudge-- [mutters].

1581
01:20:47,510 --> 01:20:48,812
-[yelling]

1582
01:20:48,878 --> 01:20:50,547
-[Crow] To the moon, Alice!

1583
01:21:01,692 --> 01:21:06,162
-[Servo] Tor could have been contender
instead bum which am.

1584
01:21:06,863 --> 01:21:09,032
-Tor have tough day.

1585
01:21:10,367 --> 01:21:13,670
-[Mike] He always does a few
push-ups before turning in.

1586
01:21:14,303 --> 01:21:16,940
-[Servo] Beautiful night.
Tor sleep on porch.

1587
01:21:19,375 --> 01:21:23,547
-[Mike grunts]
With the thing and the-- ah!

1588
01:21:23,613 --> 01:21:26,415
Yeah.
[continues grunting]

1589
01:21:30,620 --> 01:21:32,588
-[Servo] Think we can jump him, Randy?

1590
01:21:35,258 --> 01:21:38,362
-Oh, wait.
Tor forget to set alarm.

1591
01:21:39,895 --> 01:21:43,867
-[Servo] The only landscape I know
that's enhanced by telephone poles.

1592
01:21:43,933 --> 01:21:46,202
-[Mike] Would it hurt
the Atomic Energy Commission

1593
01:21:46,268 --> 01:21:48,572
to stop by once in a while to say hello?

1594
01:21:48,638 --> 01:21:51,040
-[Randy] Art, come on.

1595
01:21:53,108 --> 01:21:55,478
-[grunts]
-[Randy] Get back!

1596
01:21:55,545 --> 01:21:57,647
-[Crow] What is he, a beast,
or my mashuguna grandpa?

1597
01:21:57,713 --> 01:22:00,117
-[Servo] You kids with your bikes.
Ugh!

1598
01:22:01,517 --> 01:22:05,588
-[Mike] If Hank is gone seven years,
I'm free to marry again.

1599
01:22:06,522 --> 01:22:09,492
-[Crow] Waiting for the romance between
Tor and Mom to get going.

1600
01:22:09,558 --> 01:22:11,027
-[Mike] Yeah, that's a twist.

1601
01:22:15,065 --> 01:22:17,400
[narrator] With Hank
and some helpful neighbors...

1602
01:22:17,467 --> 01:22:19,602
-[Servo] They raise a barn.
-[narrator] ...the search narrows.

1603
01:22:19,668 --> 01:22:23,373
-[Mike] They're concentrating on the rogue
Russian scientists in the neighborhood.

1604
01:22:24,107 --> 01:22:26,442
-[Crow barking]

1605
01:22:28,177 --> 01:22:29,545
[Servo meows]

1606
01:22:29,612 --> 01:22:32,148
-I'm ready for my closeup, Mr. Francis.

1607
01:22:33,048 --> 01:22:36,585
-[Mike] Now, this could be
vacation footage, for all we know.

1608
01:22:37,853 --> 01:22:41,123
-And the Tasty Freeze staff
is brought in to help.

1609
01:22:44,693 --> 01:22:48,498
-[Servo] What are you,
with your rap, and... [groaning]

1610
01:22:49,332 --> 01:22:51,533
-[Mike] Don't turn off the game!
I was watching that!

1611
01:22:54,803 --> 01:22:56,973
-Argh, I'll teach you kids!

1612
01:22:59,142 --> 01:23:01,678
-[Servo] The tension is so thick,
you could cut it with a knife,

1613
01:23:01,745 --> 01:23:03,045
but, please, use a spoon.

1614
01:23:05,615 --> 01:23:07,750
-[Mike] Found a Playboy up here once.
Keep your eyes open.

1615
01:23:07,817 --> 01:23:08,985
-[Crow] Really?

1616
01:23:10,220 --> 01:23:12,888
-[Mike] What are you, with your
running and your [shouts].

1617
01:23:15,592 --> 01:23:19,328
-[Crow] With your cactus, and your--
argh!

1618
01:23:20,063 --> 01:23:22,698
-[Servo] Helpful neighbor
shown actual size.

1619
01:23:23,400 --> 01:23:25,568
-[Mike] That bush is trying
to get their attention.

1620
01:23:29,172 --> 01:23:31,307
-[Servo] Moses is lost in the Sinai.

1621
01:23:33,577 --> 01:23:36,713
-You forgot your complementary stick!

1622
01:23:38,113 --> 01:23:41,017
-[Mike] I had a vision I was chased
through the desert by Boog Powell.

1623
01:23:45,188 --> 01:23:47,090
[gunshot]
-[Servo] Ooh!

1624
01:23:47,323 --> 01:23:49,292
Well, it penetrated his back fat.

1625
01:23:53,095 --> 01:23:55,498
[Crow makes rumble noises]

1626
01:23:56,698 --> 01:23:58,935
-[Mike] ♪ We fired our guns
And the British kept a-comin' ♪

1627
01:23:59,002 --> 01:24:00,603
♪ From the Mississippi to-- ♪

1628
01:24:05,208 --> 01:24:08,243
-[Servo] I remember a time when there
were thousands of Tors on the plains.

1629
01:24:08,310 --> 01:24:11,080
Ground would shake.
Plains were black with Tors.

1630
01:24:11,647 --> 01:24:13,950
-Tonight, The Whales of August.

1631
01:24:14,383 --> 01:24:17,287
-[Mike] McCloud, you shot Penn Jillette.

1632
01:24:19,155 --> 01:24:21,992
-Tor is posing for a Reuben's painting.

1633
01:24:23,827 --> 01:24:26,262
-[Servo] Augh! Just kidding.

1634
01:24:27,930 --> 01:24:30,333
-[Mike] Right by
the world's largest Aryan.

1635
01:24:30,400 --> 01:24:31,433
-[Servo laughs]

1636
01:24:31,500 --> 01:24:33,168
-[Mike] Hide him.
-[Servo] Ew.

1637
01:24:35,372 --> 01:24:37,140
-[Crow] Look out! He's rolling!

1638
01:24:39,742 --> 01:24:41,210
-[Servo] Am I late for school?

1639
01:24:43,747 --> 01:24:46,115
-[Mike makes munching noises]

1640
01:24:46,148 --> 01:24:49,318
-[Servo] Another trademark
Coleman Francis butt shot.

1641
01:24:56,725 --> 01:24:59,195
-Balanchine. [giggles]

1642
01:25:01,197 --> 01:25:04,533
-[Mike] Tor's just mad because someone
ripped the Froot Loop off his shirt.

1643
01:25:05,735 --> 01:25:08,905
-[Servo] There, there.
Now try swallowing. Is that better?

1644
01:25:11,540 --> 01:25:13,877
-Tor is a field chiropractor.

1645
01:25:15,278 --> 01:25:17,113
[Mike] Close your mouth.
Quit breathing on me.

1646
01:25:17,180 --> 01:25:18,748
Please, close your mouth.

1647
01:25:21,050 --> 01:25:24,920
-[Servo laughs]
Tor like this! This fun!

1648
01:25:29,725 --> 01:25:32,495
-Hey, gotta get to bed. [laughter]

1649
01:25:32,562 --> 01:25:34,197
[gunshots]

1650
01:25:36,598 --> 01:25:38,835
[gunshots continue]

1651
01:25:42,305 --> 01:25:44,740
-[Crow] Oh, that was wonderful.

1652
01:25:45,775 --> 01:25:48,978
-[Mike] I see the belly
of the great white one!

1653
01:25:50,112 --> 01:25:51,313
-[Servo] J-Jim?

1654
01:25:51,680 --> 01:25:54,117
Now, back away slowly
from the crotch.

1655
01:25:54,183 --> 01:25:56,652
-[Joe] Jim. You all right?

1656
01:25:56,718 --> 01:26:00,022
-[Crow] They're going to split him open
and crawl inside to stay warm.

1657
01:26:00,088 --> 01:26:02,292
-[Jim] Yeah, I'm all right.

1658
01:26:02,358 --> 01:26:05,060
[Mike] Call me, Tor, hmm?

1659
01:26:05,795 --> 01:26:08,497
-[Servo] They're gonna find
a whole baby deer inside him.

1660
01:26:12,102 --> 01:26:15,272
-This has been one bleak, weird day.

1661
01:26:16,873 --> 01:26:20,410
-[Servo] Hey, about that other guy I shot?
Let's keep that quiet.

1662
01:26:21,577 --> 01:26:24,213
-Come on. Let's go.
-[Mike] We gotta find that beast.

1663
01:26:26,615 --> 01:26:30,553
-[Mike] That's the third beast this week.
We gotta start regulating them.

1664
01:26:31,720 --> 01:26:34,957
-[Servo] Slowly, Tor decays
into his base elements.

1665
01:26:35,023 --> 01:26:37,693
[narrator] Joseph Javorsky.
-[Mike] Aren't you ashamed of yourself?

1666
01:26:37,760 --> 01:26:39,162
[narrator] Noted scientist.

1667
01:26:39,228 --> 01:26:41,863
-Family man,
and your candidate for city council!

1668
01:26:47,603 --> 01:26:49,272
-[Mike] So? What about him?

1669
01:26:54,143 --> 01:26:56,212
-[Servo] Hank, I'm going back to college.

1670
01:26:59,715 --> 01:27:03,052
-[Mike] You know, women today
just can't wait like they used to.

1671
01:27:05,788 --> 01:27:08,323
-The Velveteen Rabbit.

1672
01:27:08,858 --> 01:27:11,627
-[Servo] Isn't it weird that rabbits
have ears that look like antennas?

1673
01:27:14,763 --> 01:27:15,798
-[Mike] That's Trudy.

1674
01:27:18,400 --> 01:27:21,103
-[Mike] Tell me about that last shot
of the rabbits, George.

1675
01:27:21,170 --> 01:27:23,607
-[Art] Mom?
-[Crow] Billy tried to eat my leg.

1676
01:27:23,673 --> 01:27:26,275
-[Art] Mom? Mom?

1677
01:27:26,342 --> 01:27:31,447
-[Servo fake-cries] This is so touching.
-[Mike whispers] It's OK.

1678
01:27:32,548 --> 01:27:34,383
-[Crow] Whoop-whoop-whoop.

1679
01:27:38,453 --> 01:27:42,125
-[Servo] Oh, it's a meat eating rabbit.
-[Crow] And they found the mother lode.

1680
01:27:43,960 --> 01:27:45,193
He's suckling.

1681
01:27:47,263 --> 01:27:49,132
-[Crow] Oh, uh, hi!

1682
01:27:49,198 --> 01:27:51,433
I know what this must look like.

1683
01:27:51,500 --> 01:27:54,303
-You look like my little Ninotschka.

1684
01:27:54,903 --> 01:27:58,473
-[Mike] OK, remain calm. Don't panic.

1685
01:27:59,842 --> 01:28:02,678
-[Servo] And now the bunny eats Tor
and becomes The Night of the Lepus.

1686
01:28:07,183 --> 01:28:10,152
-Hey, I got a man down over here!
Can I get a medic?

1687
01:28:10,218 --> 01:28:12,355
[Servo] Just hold on, buddy.
You're gonna be OK.

1688
01:28:14,290 --> 01:28:17,760
-[Mike] Well, nice to meet you, then.

1689
01:28:17,827 --> 01:28:20,162
-[Crow] Whoo!
That was an incredible ending.

1690
01:28:20,228 --> 01:28:22,065
I just need to sit down for a minute.

1691
01:28:36,012 --> 01:28:37,113
-Yay!
-Whoo-hoo!

1692
01:28:37,180 --> 01:28:39,248
-Oh, what a sweet victory, eh, Crow?
-Ah, sweet.

1693
01:28:39,315 --> 01:28:41,317
-We're going to bring out Mike
in just a moment,

1694
01:28:41,383 --> 01:28:42,818
but first, a little housekeeping.

1695
01:28:42,885 --> 01:28:44,720
We've got a wonderful chicken dinner
waiting for you all,

1696
01:28:44,787 --> 01:28:47,357
so if you'll just hold tight,
we just wanted to read a few

1697
01:28:47,423 --> 01:28:50,192
of the many congratulatory telegrams
that we've been receiving

1698
01:28:50,258 --> 01:28:51,993
over the course of the campaign.
Crow?

1699
01:28:52,062 --> 01:28:55,030
-Ah, here's a congratulary, uh--
Here we go, right here.

1700
01:28:55,097 --> 01:28:58,968
This is from Erica Rodriguez Fletcher.
And Erica writes,

1701
01:28:59,035 --> 01:29:02,472
"Your show has been a part of our lives
since I was in the fourth grade."

1702
01:29:02,538 --> 01:29:04,440
-[Crow] Wow! Wow!
-"I'm in high school now."

1703
01:29:04,507 --> 01:29:06,842
-"And we still watch your show."
-Wow!

1704
01:29:06,908 --> 01:29:09,578
-"Makes me happy to see our family
laugh their heads off together."

1705
01:29:09,645 --> 01:29:11,447
-Wow!
-"We have a pretty stressful life,

1706
01:29:11,513 --> 01:29:13,917
but you relieve it.
-From us all."

1707
01:29:13,983 --> 01:29:15,652
Ah, thank you. Thank you.
-Isn't that sweet?

1708
01:29:15,718 --> 01:29:17,620
-Thank you, Erica.
-Very sweet.

1709
01:29:17,687 --> 01:29:18,988
--Here's one from Philip White.
--Philip White.

1710
01:29:19,055 --> 01:29:20,857
-White. White. "Greetings, you all.

1711
01:29:20,923 --> 01:29:24,460
I'm not young, artistic, and
precocious, and as not such,

1712
01:29:24,527 --> 01:29:26,628
would not have a snowball's
chance in Minnesota

1713
01:29:26,695 --> 01:29:29,098
of having my letter put on Still Store."
-What?

1714
01:29:29,165 --> 01:29:31,900
-I don't quite get that, but let's see
if we can put-- Oh, no, I'm sorry.

1715
01:29:31,967 --> 01:29:33,435
We can't put it on Still Store. Sorry.

1716
01:29:33,502 --> 01:29:35,170
-Can't put it up there.
-But wait, here comes Mike!

1717
01:29:35,237 --> 01:29:37,773
-It's Mike, everybody!
-Mike Nelson, Mike Nelson!

1718
01:29:37,840 --> 01:29:40,208
Thank you, thank you very much.

1719
01:29:40,275 --> 01:29:41,610
Mike, Mike, Mike, Mike!

1720
01:29:41,677 --> 01:29:43,847
-Thank you. Thank you.

1721
01:29:43,913 --> 01:29:46,348
Thank you very much.
And thank you.

1722
01:29:46,415 --> 01:29:47,750
Thank you. And thank you.

1723
01:29:47,817 --> 01:29:52,322
It was a long and hard campaign,
yet we emerged victorious.

1724
01:29:52,388 --> 01:29:53,555
-[robots] Yay!

1725
01:29:53,622 --> 01:29:55,525
-We tamed The Beast of Yucca Flats.

1726
01:29:55,592 --> 01:29:56,658
-[cheering]

1727
01:29:56,725 --> 01:29:59,462
-We rallied around
the Coleman Francis cry.

1728
01:29:59,528 --> 01:30:01,363
-I'm Cherokee Jack.

1729
01:30:01,430 --> 01:30:04,433
-We took the worst they had to offer,
and we say proudly--

1730
01:30:04,500 --> 01:30:05,635
-Coffee?

1731
01:30:05,702 --> 01:30:07,403
Yes.
I like coffee.

1732
01:30:07,470 --> 01:30:09,905
-With my wife Nancy by my side,

1733
01:30:09,972 --> 01:30:13,142
we overcame a generation
forged in war and discipline

1734
01:30:13,208 --> 01:30:16,945
by a hard and bitter piece of Harry,
England and Saint George.

1735
01:30:17,012 --> 01:30:19,315
We have been to the mountaintop,

1736
01:30:19,348 --> 01:30:21,517
and we heard it say
"Ich ben ein Berlinner,"

1737
01:30:21,583 --> 01:30:23,853
and "Ain't I a woman?"

1738
01:30:28,390 --> 01:30:32,962
-First of all, I'd like to say

1739
01:30:33,028 --> 01:30:37,767
that what we've suffered here tonight
is not a defeat.

1740
01:30:37,833 --> 01:30:39,768
Definitely not a defeat.

1741
01:30:40,035 --> 01:30:43,438
It may look like defeat.

1742
01:30:43,505 --> 01:30:46,575
It may feel like defeat.

1743
01:30:47,177 --> 01:30:49,878
It may even smell like defeat.

1744
01:30:50,947 --> 01:30:52,415
Oh, what the heck.

1745
01:30:52,915 --> 01:30:55,952
Frank, I'm going to
start slapping you now,

1746
01:30:56,018 --> 01:30:57,887
and I may never stop.

1747
01:30:59,355 --> 01:31:01,290
-Let the healing process begin.

1748
01:31:05,360 --> 01:31:07,330
[theme music interposed with slapping]

1749
01:32:09,658 --> 01:32:12,328
-[yelling]
